-- 073: nine articles that cited nothing, and in places cited things that
-- do not exist.
--
-- Cercol's position is that it is built on peer-reviewed research and that
-- every number is citable. These nine broke it. Each presented precise
-- correlation coefficients in prominent stat cards with no DOI anywhere in
-- the article and, in most, no Sources section either. 25 cards, 0 citations.
--
-- Three research psychologists were told their default verdict was
-- "unverified", to prefer meta-analyses, and to read an abstract before
-- citing it rather than trust a search snippet. What they found is worse
-- than absent sourcing.
--
-- Numbers that are wrong, not merely unsourced:
--
--   Extraversion and negotiation was given as r = 0.28 with assertiveness.
--   Barry & Friedman (1998), the direct test, reports -0.26 with the
--   negotiator's own economic gain. Openness and integrative outcomes was
--   given as +0.19; the same paper reports -0.31. Opposite signs.
--
--   Person-environment fit and turnover intention was given as +0.26.
--   Kristof-Brown et al. (2005) reports -0.46. Sign and size both.
--
--   Conscientiousness predicting GPA was given as 0.73. Poropat (2009),
--   k = 138, N = 70,926, reports rho = 0.22. Off by more than three times.
--
--   Neuroticism was said to relate negatively to avoiding in conflict.
--   Antonioni (1998) and Tehrani & Yamini (2020) both report it positively.
--
-- Attributions to work that does not say it:
--
--   A block quotation attributed to Lencioni that does not exist, plus
--   findings attributed to Harms et al. (2011), Gillath et al. (2010) and
--   Rotter (1980) that none of those papers make.
--
--   A block quotation attributed to Ones, Viswesvaran & Reiss (1996) that
--   does not exist, in a paper whose title calls that very concern a red
--   herring.
--
--   A block quotation attributed to Viswesvaran & Ones (1999), citing the
--   wrong journal.
--
--   A block quotation attributed to De Dreu & Weingart (2003) that is not in
--   the paper, supporting a claim the paper contradicts: it measured
--   conflict amount, not styles, and found no positive correlation with
--   performance anywhere.
--
--   Five findings in the feedback article attributed to London & Smither
--   (1995), Ashford & Blatt (2003), Anseel & Lievens (2007) and Smither et
--   al. (2005), which contain none of them. The word "agreeableness" does
--   not appear in the Smither paper at all.
--
--   Graziano et al. (1996) cited for Openness. The paper is about
--   Agreeableness.
--
-- What was done. A card whose value could be sourced was corrected to the
-- published figure and attributed. A card that could not be sourced was
-- removed, and the argument around it rewritten to make the qualitative
-- claim the evidence does support rather than left with a hole. Where a
-- recommendation survives without evidence, the text now says so.
--
-- 25 cards to 8. Zero DOIs to between three and six per article. Every
-- article is longer, because an explanation of what is actually known takes
-- more room than a number nobody stood behind.
--
-- All 39 proposed DOIs were checked against Crossref independently of the
-- agents that proposed them: every one resolves, and the author, year and
-- title match what was claimed for it.
--
-- The five translations of these nine articles are now stale against a
-- corrected English and are retranslated separately. Until that lands, the
-- wrong-signed coefficients are still live in five languages.

BEGIN;

UPDATE blog_posts SET content = jsonb_set(content, '{en}', '"# 16Personalities vs Big Five: the test that''s half right\n\nIf you have ever sent a personality test link to a friend, there is a reasonable chance it pointed to [16Personalities.com](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/16Personalities). The site has been taken by hundreds of millions of people worldwide, offers its results for free, and generates shareable type descriptions that resonate with a huge range of readers. It is arguably the most culturally visible personality assessment on the internet.\n\nBut popularity and validity are not the same thing. This article examines what 16Personalities actually measures, where it succeeds, where it falls short, and what the scientific literature, including the [Big Five](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Five_personality_traits) framework, tells us about it.\n\n---\n\n## What Is 16Personalities and How Does It Relate to MBTI?\n\n<div class=\"stat-grid\">\n  <div class=\"stat-card\">\n    <div class=\"stat-value\">1.5B+</div>\n    <div class=\"stat-label\">tests taken, 16Personalities'' own published counter</div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"stat-card\">\n    <div class=\"stat-value\">ρ = 0.22</div>\n    <div class=\"stat-label\">Big Five Conscientiousness and academic performance</div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n16Personalities is a free online personality assessment created by NERIS Analytics. It draws heavily from the [Myers-Briggs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myers%E2%80%93Briggs_Type_Indicator) framework, the same four binary dimensions (E/I, S/N, T/F, J/P), but adds a fifth axis called Identity: Assertive (A) vs. Turbulent (T). That fifth axis brings the total to 32 possible profiles, though the site still groups outputs into 16 named types (Architect, Campaigner, Mediator, and so on), each assigned a four-letter MBTI-style code.\n\nThe two figures above are not the same kind of number, and the difference is the point of this article. The first is a running counter that 16Personalities publishes on its own homepage, where it currently reads more than 1.5 billion tests taken. It comes from the company, not from an independent audit, and it measures reach rather than accuracy. The second comes from a meta-analysis of 138 independent samples and 70,926 people, in which Big Five Conscientiousness, the dimension Cèrcol calls Discipline, correlated with academic performance at a corrected r of .22, with an observed correlation of .19 [doi:10.1037/a0014996](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0014996). One of these numbers tells you how many people took a test. The other tells you what a trait score is worth.\n\nThe test is free, takes about twelve minutes, and produces richly written, visually designed type descriptions. That combination, free, fast, visually appealing, emotionally resonant, explains most of its virality. The descriptions are written broadly enough to feel personally accurate to most readers, a well-documented phenomenon in psychology known as the Barnum or Forer effect.\n\n---\n\n## How 16Personalities Became the World''s Most-Taken Personality Test\n\n16Personalities consistently ranks among the most-visited psychology-adjacent websites in the world. It has been translated into dozens of languages. Workplace use is common: teams share their types in onboarding documents, Slack bios, and team retrospectives. Its cultural footprint is substantial, \"INFJ\" and \"ENFP\" have become common identity markers in online communities.\n\nThis reach is worth acknowledging seriously. When a tool becomes this embedded in how people talk about themselves, dismissing it entirely is neither accurate nor useful. Something about it is working.\n\n---\n\n## The Scientific Problems With 16Personalities'' Type System\n\nThe core scientific problems with 16Personalities are inherited from MBTI, which it closely resembles:\n\n**1. Binary types vs. continuous traits.** The most empirically well-supported finding in personality science is that personality traits are normally distributed across populations: they are spectrums, not categories. Forcing a continuous dimension like Extraversion/Introversion into a binary creates a false discontinuity. This has been tested directly on the MBTI itself. McCrae and Costa evaluated the instrument against the five-factor model in 468 adults and found no support for the view that it measures truly dichotomous preferences or qualitatively distinct types; what it measures is four relatively independent continuous dimensions [doi:10.1111/j.1467-6494.1989.tb00759.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-6494.1989.tb00759.x). People who score near the midpoint on any dimension will be arbitrarily assigned to one side or the other, and may receive a completely different type on retesting. For a fuller treatment of why this matters, see [what is reliability and validity in personality testing](/blog/what-is-reliability-validity-in-personality-testing).\n\n**2. Test-retest reliability.** Because of the binary categorisation problem, a meaningful percentage of people receive different four-letter codes when they retake the test weeks later. The same issue documented with MBTI (see our article on [MBTI vs Big Five](/blog/mbti-vs-big-five-which-should-your-team-use)) applies here. A test that does not reproduce its own results is not measuring a stable trait.\n\n**3. Limited predictive validity.** This article quotes no validity coefficient for 16Personalities, and that absence is deliberate: there is no substantial independent peer-reviewed literature reporting one. NERIS Analytics has not published external validation studies in the peer-reviewed record, and no independent replication of the instrument''s structure or its criterion validity exists to cite. Contrast that with the Big Five, where Conscientiousness alone has been meta-analysed across 138 independent samples for academic performance [doi:10.1037/a0014996](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0014996). The honest statement about 16Personalities is not that it predicts poorly; it is that nobody independent has measured whether it predicts at all. See [personality science: evidence-based HR and why it matters](/blog/personality-science-evidence-based-hr-why-it-matters).\n\n**4. The Barnum effect in written descriptions.** The type descriptions on 16Personalities are written to be broadly applicable. Research on the Barnum effect (Forer, 1949) shows that people accept vague, positive personality descriptions as uniquely accurate for themselves even when the same description would apply to most people. This does not mean the descriptions are wrong, but it means personal resonance is not evidence of measurement validity. For more on myths like this one, see [five personality science myths that won''t die](/blog/five-personality-science-myths-that-wont-die).\n\n---\n\n## Where 16Personalities Gets the Big Five Partially Right\n\n16Personalities is not pure noise. It is built on four axes that a peer-reviewed literature has already shown to carry real trait variance.\n\n**The Identity axis (A vs. T) is the one genuinely new dimension.** The Assertive/Turbulent axis is described by NERIS as capturing confidence and emotional reactivity, which places it conceptually where the Big Five puts emotional stability, the inverse of Neuroticism (Depth in Cèrcol). That is a sensible place to add a fifth axis, since the four MBTI-derived dimensions leave Neuroticism uncovered. It should be said plainly that this mapping has not been tested in an independent study: it is a reading of what the axis is designed to do, not a measured correlation.\n\n**The four inherited dimensions have partial Big Five overlap.** This is the part that does rest on published evidence. McCrae and Costa correlated the four MBTI indices with the NEO Personality Inventory, using self-reports and peer ratings from 468 adults, and found that each index measured aspects of four of the five major dimensions of normal personality [doi:10.1111/j.1467-6494.1989.tb00759.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-6494.1989.tb00759.x). Because 16Personalities uses the same four axes, that result is the best available evidence for the correspondence set out below.\n\n| 16Personalities dimension | Closest Big Five mapping |\n|---|---|\n| Extraversion (E) vs. Introversion (I) | Extraversion / Presence |\n| Intuition (N) vs. Sensing (S) | Openness to Experience / Vision (partially) |\n| Thinking (T) vs. Feeling (F) | Agreeableness / Bond (inversely) |\n| Judging (J) vs. Perceiving (P) | Conscientiousness / Discipline (partially) |\n| Assertive (A) vs. Turbulent (T) | Emotional Stability (inverse of Neuroticism / Depth) |\n\nThe table is a structural correspondence, not a table of coefficients. Each axis conflates variance from more than one Big Five factor, and the fifth row is the least evidenced of the five. But the overlap is not zero, and it explains why people generally recognise themselves in the Extraversion half of their code.\n\n**It is a gateway.** For many people, 16Personalities is their first encounter with the idea that personality can be measured and discussed systematically. That is genuinely valuable. The conversation it opens matters.\n\n---\n\n## Types vs Trait Spectrums: Why the Distinction Matters for Teams\n\nThis distinction is worth dwelling on because it underlies most of the scientific criticism of MBTI-adjacent tools.\n\nA **type** says: you are either X or Y. You are an Introvert or an Extravert. You are an INTJ or an INFJ.\n\nA **trait spectrum** says: you fall somewhere on a continuous distribution, and your exact position on that distribution predicts specific outcomes in specific contexts.\n\nThe type approach is more memorable and generates better stories. The spectrum approach is more accurate and more useful for decisions. Someone who scores 55 on Extraversion and someone who scores 80 will both be typed as \"Extravert,\" but they will behave very differently in a high-stimulation work environment. That difference matters for team design.\n\nUnderstanding facet-level structure amplifies this further, see [what is a facet in personality psychology](/blog/what-is-a-facet-in-personality-psychology) for how sub-trait resolution adds information that any type system structurally cannot capture.\n\n---\n\n## When to Use 16Personalities, and When Big Five Is the Better Choice\n\n16Personalities is a genuinely excellent conversation starter. It gives people accessible language to discuss their preferences, working styles, and social needs. In a low-stakes context, a team offsite, an onboarding icebreaker, a personal reflection exercise, it can be valuable.\n\nWhere it should not be used: hiring decisions, performance evaluation, team composition for high-stakes projects, coaching programmes that require tracking change over time. For those purposes, you need a tool with stable scores, dimensional resolution, and a published validity record. The Big Five, measured through IPIP-based instruments, provides all three. See [the best free personality tests for teams in 2026](/blog/best-free-personality-tests-for-teams-2026) for a ranked comparison that includes 16Personalities.\n\nIf your team has been using 16Personalities as a shared language, you do not have to abandon it entirely. But layering in a proper Big Five assessment, one that gives each person a continuous profile across five validated dimensions, will add information that 16Personalities simply cannot provide.\n\n---\n\n## 16Personalities vs Big Five: try the validated alternative free\n\nIf 16Personalities has been your team''s shared language, it has done something useful: it has made personality a topic you can talk about. The next step is to replace the binary type labels with continuous, facet-level scores that come with published criterion validity.\n\nCèrcol measures all five Big Five dimensions, including Depth (Neuroticism), which 16Personalities'' A/T axis approximates but does not fully capture, using 120 items drawn from the open-domain [IPIP](https://ipip.ori.org) item bank. Each person gets a continuous profile, not a four-letter code, meaning the information is preserved rather than collapsed. The [Witness peer assessment](https://cercol.team/instruments) adds an outside perspective, surfacing the blind spots that self-report alone will always miss.\n\nThe assessment is free. It takes about 15 minutes. And unlike 16Personalities, the scoring is transparent, auditable, and grounded in a scientific literature that spans five decades and 50-plus countries. Start at [cercol.team](https://cercol.team), or read about [the science behind the instrument](https://cercol.team/science) first.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Poropat, A. E. (2009). A meta-analysis of the five-factor model of personality and academic performance. *Psychological Bulletin.* [https://doi.org/10.1037/a0014996](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0014996)\n- McCrae, R. R., & Costa, P. T. (1989). Reinterpreting the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator from the perspective of the five-factor model of personality. *Journal of Personality.* [https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-6494.1989.tb00759.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-6494.1989.tb00759.x)\n- 16Personalities, tests-taken counter published by NERIS Analytics: [https://www.16personalities.com](https://www.16personalities.com)\n- Wikipedia: [16Personalities](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/16Personalities)\n- Wikipedia: [Big Five personality traits](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Five_personality_traits)\n- Wikipedia: [Myers-Briggs Type Indicator](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myers%E2%80%93Briggs_Type_Indicator)\n- IPIP: International Personality Item Pool: [https://ipip.ori.org](https://ipip.ori.org)\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [MBTI vs Big Five: which one should your team use?](/blog/mbti-vs-big-five-which-should-your-team-use)\n- [DISC vs Big Five: why four styles aren''t enough](/blog/disc-vs-big-five-why-four-styles-arent-enough)\n- [Big Five vs DISC vs Belbin: a scientist''s comparison](/blog/big-five-vs-disc-vs-belbin)\n- [Can you fake a personality test?](/blog/can-you-fake-a-personality-test)\n- [What is reliability and validity in personality testing?](/blog/what-is-reliability-validity-in-personality-testing)\n- [Five personality science myths that won''t die](/blog/five-personality-science-myths-that-wont-die)\n"'::jsonb),
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE slug = '16personalities-vs-big-five-the-viral-test-that-gets-it-half-right';
UPDATE blog_posts SET content = jsonb_set(content, '{en}', '"# Anonymity in personality assessment: why it matters\n\nWhen you complete a personality questionnaire, you are not operating in a vacuum. You know, consciously or not, who is going to see the results. And that knowledge changes what you say.\n\nThis is one of the most robust and underappreciated findings in the personality measurement literature. The social context of an assessment, who has commissioned it, who will have access to the results, what consequences may follow, systematically shapes responses, often in ways that are invisible to both the respondent and the person interpreting the scores. A general introduction to the concept and its implications is available at [Wikipedia: Anonymity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anonymity).\n\nAssessment administrators frequently acknowledge this problem in the abstract while underestimating it in practice. Understanding the research on anonymity and social desirability bias is essential for anyone who wants to use personality data responsibly, whether for personal development, team understanding, or organisational purposes.\n\n---\n\n## Social Desirability Bias: Why Assessment Context Distorts Scores\n\nSocial desirability bias is the tendency for people to answer questions in ways they believe will be viewed favourably by others. In personality assessment, it manifests as systematic score inflation on traits perceived as positive, Conscientiousness, Agreeableness, Extraversion, and systematic score deflation on traits perceived as negative, Neuroticism, and sometimes certain facets of Openness. For a comprehensive treatment of how this bias operates, see [social desirability bias in personality tests](/blog/social-desirability-bias-personality-tests).\n\nThe effect is not uniform across individuals. The literature distinguishes two components of socially desirable responding: self-deception, the genuine but inflated belief in one''s own positive qualities, and impression management, the deliberate presentation of a favourable self-image. Self-deception is relatively stable across contexts; impression management is highly sensitive to the assessment context.\n\nThe key driver of impression management is perceived stakes. When assessments have low stakes, when you believe the results are purely for your own reflection, impression management is minimal. When assessments have high stakes, when you believe results may affect hiring decisions, performance evaluations, or access to opportunities, impression management increases. The content of the questionnaire has not changed; the social context has.\n\nHow large is the shift? The best available estimate comes from a meta-analysis of 33 studies that compared the scores of real job applicants with the scores of non-applicants on the same measures. Applicants scored higher on conscientiousness (d = .45), emotional stability (d = .44), openness (d = .13) and extraversion (d = .11) [doi:10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x). Agreeableness was not among the dimensions showing a significant applicant advantage. Roughly speaking, then, the two dimensions employers care about most move by a little under half a standard deviation when the context changes from research to selection, and the other three barely move at all.\n\n---\n\n## Anonymous vs Identified Conditions: What the Research Shows\n\nA consistent experimental literature has examined how personality scores change when the same respondents complete the same questionnaire under different disclosure conditions.\n\n**Anonymous conditions**, where participants are told that their individual responses cannot be linked to their identity, are associated with less distortion. A meta-analysis of 61 studies and 673 effect sizes covering computerised questionnaires, paper questionnaires and face-to-face interviews found that social desirability distortion was small specifically when respondents were alone, anonymous, and able to backtrack through their answers [doi:10.1037/0021-9010.84.5.754](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.84.5.754). Anonymity in that analysis is one condition among several that jointly determine how private an assessment feels; it is not a switch that turns bias off on its own, and no study puts a single percentage on how much it removes.\n\n**Identified conditions** with high-stakes framing, where participants know results will be seen by an employer or a selection committee, produce the largest inflation. The applicant meta-analysis above is the direct measurement of this: conscientiousness at d = .45 and emotional stability at d = .44 relative to non-applicant samples [doi:10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x). Because emotional stability is the inverse pole of Neuroticism, the same result is a suppression of Neuroticism of the same size, which is why the figure below is expressed in the terms the meta-analysis used.\n\n**Developmental conditions**, where participants are told that results are for their own learning and will not be shared without consent, sit between anonymous and selection contexts. Telling someone \"this is for your development\" reduces but does not eliminate impression management, because participants still interact with a human administrator or platform who they perceive as capable of judgement. The magnitude of that intermediate case has not been meta-analysed, so it is described here qualitatively rather than with a coefficient.\n\nThe practical implication is straightforward: if you want to know what someone is actually like, the context under which you assess them matters as much as the instrument you use. This same logic underlies why [peer assessment is more reliable than self-report alone](/blog/why-self-assessment-alone-isnt-enough-peer-personality-feedback), peers observe actual behaviour rather than managed impressions.\n\n<div class=\"stat-grid\">\n  <div class=\"stat-card\" style=\"border-top: 3px solid #cf3339;\">\n    <div class=\"stat-value\" style=\"color: #cf3339;\">d = 0.44</div>\n    <div class=\"stat-label\">Neuroticism suppression, job applicants vs non-applicants</div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## How High-Stakes Contexts Inflate Personality Scores in Hiring\n\nThe stakes problem is particularly acute in employment selection, where personality assessment has become widespread. When candidates know, or suspect, that their personality scores will influence hiring decisions, they have a strong incentive to present themselves as highly Conscientious, reliably Agreeable, and emotionally stable. The research shows they do the first and the third. For the full treatment of deliberate distortion, see [can you fake a personality test?](/blog/can-you-fake-a-personality-test).\n\nThis creates a systematic problem for selection use cases. The scores that organisations collect in high-stakes selection contexts are not the same as the scores they would collect from the same individuals in a low-stakes developmental context. They represent a mixture of genuine trait expression and deliberate self-presentation. How to disentangle these is not obvious, and the psychometric techniques that exist for detecting and correcting faking, forced-choice formats, consistency checks, social desirability scales, all have significant limitations.\n\nSome assessment providers have responded by arguing that applicant faking does not matter, because the scores still predict performance even when inflated. This argument has real empirical support. A meta-analysis of the social desirability literature found that social desirability scales did not predict job performance, school success, task performance or counterproductive behaviours, and that social desirability does not function as a useful suppressor or mediator for job performance; removing its effects from the Big Five dimensions leaves their workplace validity intact [doi:10.1037/0021-9010.81.6.660](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.81.6.660). A later study went further and questioned how much faking actually happens: among 5,266 rejected applicants who reapplied for the same job six months later and retook the same five-factor measure, 5.2% or fewer improved their scores on any scale, and changes were as likely to go down as up [doi:10.1037/0021-9010.92.5.1270](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.92.5.1270).\n\nThat evidence sidesteps the core problem rather than solving it. If you are trying to understand someone''s genuine personality for developmental or team-building purposes, inflated scores actively mislead, whether or not they still correlate with a performance criterion. The argument that faking does not matter for prediction holds most plausibly in selection contexts with clear performance criteria; it holds much less well when the purpose is genuine personality understanding.\n\nFor further analysis of the legal and ethical dimensions of personality assessment in employment, see [personality testing in hiring: what is legal and what is ethical](/blog/personality-testing-in-hiring-what-is-legal-what-is-ethical).\n\n---\n\n## Why Assessment Transparency Produces More Honest Personality Data\n\nIf high-stakes contexts inflate scores, one response is to reduce the stakes. This means reframing who owns the data and what it can be used for.\n\nThe argument for assessment transparency rests on informed consent and individual ownership. When you complete a personality assessment and retain full control over who sees the results, and can be confident that the results are not accessible to your employer, your manager, or anyone who could use them against you, you have a strong reason to answer honestly. The social desirability calculation changes: there is no audience to impress.\n\nIt is worth being precise about which kind of transparency does the work. Simply warning respondents that faking is detectable has a measurable but small effect: a review of the warning literature put it at d = 0.23, with warned respondents scoring lower than unwarned ones, and the size of the effect depending on the type of warning used [doi:10.1207/S15327043HUP1601_1](https://doi.org/10.1207/S15327043HUP1601_1). Structural transparency, designing assessment systems where individuals genuinely own and control their data, aims at the incentive rather than the warning. When you know that no one but you sees the results unless you choose to share them, the optimal strategy for impression management is simply to be accurate.\n\nThis is not a naive view. People still have self-presentation motives that operate even in genuinely private contexts, self-deception remains. But eliminating the external audience eliminates the largest driver of deliberate faking. The item design also matters: [forced-choice formats](/blog/forced-choice-personality-assessment-more-honest-data) remove the ability to simply endorse all positive descriptors, working alongside anonymity to improve data quality.\n\n---\n\n## Cèrcol''s Design: Individual Data Ownership as Accuracy Strategy\n\nCèrcol is designed around the principle that personality results belong to the person being assessed, not to an organisation, employer, or platform. This is not a secondary feature: it is a foundational architectural decision that flows directly from the research on anonymity and social desirability.\n\nWhen individuals know that their Cèrcol profile is theirs to control, that it will not be visible to their employer unless they actively choose to share it, that Witness assessments are mediated through a consent framework that they control, the assessment context shifts from high-stakes to genuinely developmental. The research predicts that scores obtained in this context will be more honest, more accurate, and more useful than scores obtained in a conventional selection or evaluation context.\n\nThe peer assessment model, in which Witnesses who know the individual complete their own assessment of the individual''s personality, adds a further dimension of accuracy by introducing independent perspectives that are less susceptible to the individual''s own self-presentation biases. Self-deception is hard to sustain against a converging set of assessments from people who see you in context. This is described in full in [what the Cèrcol Witness instrument measures](/blog/what-the-cercol-witness-instrument-measures).\n\n---\n\n## Assessment Condition vs Score Quality: A Comparison\n\n| Assessment condition | Typical score inflation | Trust implication |\n|---|---|---|\n| Anonymous research (no identification possible) | Minimal, baseline for trait measurement | Highest validity for research purposes |\n| Developmental (individual sees results only) | Low, modest self-presentational motive remains; not meta-analysed | Good for genuine self-understanding |\n| Employer-commissioned, developmental framing | Moderate, depends on organisational trust; not meta-analysed | Scores reflect mixture of trait and context |\n| High-stakes selection (employer sees results) | Around d = .45 on conscientiousness and emotional stability | Scores require significant interpretive caution |\n| Coerced or mandatory organisational assessment | Not separately measured; incentive to manage impression is strongest here | Scores should be treated with scepticism |\n\nThe one row carrying a number is the one the literature has measured [doi:10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x). The others are ordered by the strength of the incentive to manage impressions, not by measured effect sizes, and should be read that way.\n\n---\n\n## Best Practice: Four Principles for Responsible Personality Assessment\n\nThe evidence on anonymity and social desirability points to several clear principles for responsible personality assessment.\n\n**Individual data ownership.** Results should belong to the assessed individual by default. Sharing should require explicit, revocable consent. The assessed person should always be able to see their own results before anyone else does.\n\n**Transparent purpose.** The stated purpose of the assessment should be accurate. \"This is for your development\" should not be a reassuring cover for evaluation data that will be used in performance reviews or promotion decisions. Purpose deception is both ethically wrong and practically counterproductive, it destroys the trust that makes honest responding possible.\n\n**Low-stakes framing.** Wherever possible, personality assessment should be positioned as a tool for personal insight rather than organisational judgement. Organisations that want genuine personality data, rather than impression management profiles, should invest in building the trust conditions under which honest responding becomes rational.\n\n**Awareness of context effects.** Anyone interpreting personality scores should know the conditions under which those scores were obtained. A Conscientiousness score obtained in a selection context and a Conscientiousness score obtained in an anonymous developmental context are not equivalent measurements, and the applicant literature puts the gap at close to half a standard deviation. Treating them as interchangeable is an interpretive error. For background on what makes a measurement valid in the first place, see [what is reliability and validity in personality testing?](/blog/what-is-reliability-validity-in-personality-testing).\n\n## Why Cèrcol uses anonymous peer ratings\n\nThe research reviewed here leads to a clear design conclusion: anonymous, low-stakes contexts produce the most honest personality data. Cèrcol is built on this principle from the ground up. Witness peer ratings are collected anonymously, raters know their individual responses are not attributable, and the assessed individual owns their own profile, sharing it only when they choose to. This removes the primary incentive for impression management on both sides. Combined with a [forced-choice item format](/blog/forced-choice-personality-assessment-more-honest-data) that makes it structurally difficult to fake, Cèrcol''s design systematically addresses the biases that undermine conventional assessments. You can experience this directly, the full assessment is free at [cercol.team](https://cercol.team). Learn more about how the Witness instrument works at [/instruments](/instruments).\n\n---\n\n*Further reading: [Social desirability bias in personality tests](/blog/social-desirability-bias-personality-tests) · [Personality testing in hiring: what is legal, what is ethical](/blog/personality-testing-in-hiring-what-is-legal-what-is-ethical)*\n\n## Sources\n\n- Birkeland, S. A., Manson, T. M., Kisamore, J. L., Brannick, M. T., & Smith, M. A. (2006). A meta-analytic investigation of job applicant faking on personality measures. *International Journal of Selection and Assessment.* [https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x)\n- Richman, W. L., Kiesler, S., Weisband, S., & Drasgow, F. (1999). A meta-analytic study of social desirability distortion in computer-administered questionnaires, traditional questionnaires, and interviews. *Journal of Applied Psychology.* [https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.84.5.754](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.84.5.754)\n- Ones, D. S., Viswesvaran, C., & Reiss, A. D. (1996). Role of social desirability in personality testing for personnel selection: The red herring. *Journal of Applied Psychology.* [https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.81.6.660](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.81.6.660)\n- Hogan, J., Barrett, P., & Hogan, R. (2007). Personality measurement, faking, and employment selection. *Journal of Applied Psychology.* [https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.92.5.1270](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.92.5.1270)\n- Dwight, S. A., & Donovan, J. J. (2003). Do warnings not to fake reduce faking? *Human Performance.* [https://doi.org/10.1207/S15327043HUP1601_1](https://doi.org/10.1207/S15327043HUP1601_1)\n- Wikipedia: [Anonymity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anonymity)\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [Social Desirability Bias in Personality Tests](/blog/social-desirability-bias-personality-tests)\n- [Can You Fake a Personality Test](/blog/can-you-fake-a-personality-test)\n- [How Peer Assessment Works in Personality Testing](/blog/why-self-assessment-alone-isnt-enough-peer-personality-feedback)\n- [Self-Other Agreement in Personality Assessments](/blog/self-other-agreement-big-five-where-gaps-are-biggest)\n- [Personality Testing in Hiring: Ethics and Best Practices](/blog/personality-testing-in-hiring-what-is-legal-what-is-ethical)\n- [What the Cèrcol Witness Instrument Measures](/blog/what-the-cercol-witness-instrument-measures)"'::jsonb),
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE slug = 'anonymity-in-personality-assessment-why-it-matters';
UPDATE blog_posts SET content = jsonb_set(content, '{en}', '"# Can you fake a personality test, and does it matter?\n\nIf you have ever taken a personality test as part of a job application, you have probably wondered: what are they actually looking for? And if you knew what they were looking for, could you just answer accordingly?\n\nThe honest answer to the first question is: yes, there are patterns employers tend to prefer, higher Conscientiousness, moderate to high Extraversion for many roles, lower Neuroticism. The honest answer to the second is more complicated.\n\nThis article reviews what the scientific literature actually says about faking on personality tests, how much it happens, whether it matters for predictive validity, and what it tells us about the appropriate use of personality assessment.\n\n## Can People Fake Personality Tests? Yes: When Instructed\n\nThe fakeability research is unambiguous on one point: when people are explicitly instructed to present themselves as ideal candidates, they can and do shift their scores. The definitive meta-analysis pooled 51 studies of instructed faking and found that fakability did not vary by personality dimension, all five Big Five factors were equally fakable, and that distortion was largest on social desirability scales rather than on the trait scales themselves [doi:10.1177/00131649921969802](https://doi.org/10.1177/00131649921969802). Instructions to fake good produced smaller effects than instructions to fake bad, and within-subjects designs produced the more accurate estimates.\n\nTwo things are worth noting about that result. The first is what it does not say: it does not license a single headline number for \"how much people inflate\", because the estimate depends heavily on design, and the paper''s own conclusion is that between-subjects designs distort the figure. The second is that instructed faking is a laboratory ceiling, not a description of hiring. The meta-analysis of real job applicants below found smaller mean differences than the instructed-faking studies did, and said so explicitly [doi:10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x).\n\nThis is not surprising. The [social desirability bias](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_desirability_bias), the tendency to answer questions in ways that present a favourable social image, is one of the best-documented phenomena in survey research. Personality tests are not immune to it. For a broader treatment of how assessment context shapes scores, see [anonymity in personality assessment: why it matters](/blog/anonymity-in-personality-assessment-why-it-matters).\n\n<div class=\"stat-grid\">\n  <div class=\"stat-card\">\n    <div class=\"stat-value\">d = 0.45</div>\n    <div class=\"stat-label\">conscientiousness inflation, real job applicants vs non-applicants</div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"stat-card\" style=\"border-top: 3px solid #427c42;\">\n    <div class=\"stat-value\" style=\"color: #427c42;\">d = 0.06</div>\n    <div class=\"stat-label\">score inflation on forced-choice measures in high-stakes settings</div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\nThose two figures bracket the practical question. The first is what a conventional Likert-scale measure loses when the stakes go up, from a meta-analysis of 33 applicant samples [doi:10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x). The second is what a forced-choice measure loses under the same pressure, from a meta-analysis of studies comparing forced-choice scores across low-stakes and high-stakes conditions [doi:10.1037/apl0000414](https://doi.org/10.1037/apl0000414). Both sections below unpack them.\n\n## How Much Faking Actually Happens in Real Hiring Contexts\n\nHere is where the picture becomes more nuanced. The laboratory evidence for faking under instruction is robust. The evidence for spontaneous, uninstructed faking in actual hiring contexts is weaker.\n\nThe relevant meta-analysis pooled 33 studies comparing job applicants with non-applicants on the same personality measures. Applicants scored higher on conscientiousness (d = .45), emotional stability (d = .44), openness (d = .13) and extraversion (d = .11); agreeableness was not among the dimensions showing a significant applicant advantage, and the pattern shifted by job type, with applicants distorting most on the dimensions most obviously relevant to the job they wanted [doi:10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x). So the differences are real, concentrated on two of the five dimensions, and smaller than instructed faking implies.\n\nA large retest study pushes further in the same direction. Among 5,266 rejected applicants who reapplied for the same job six months later and retook the same five-factor measure, 5.2% or fewer improved their scores on any scale, scores were as likely to move down as up, and only three people improved across all five scales beyond a 95% confidence threshold [doi:10.1037/0021-9010.92.5.1270](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.92.5.1270). People who had every reason to game the test on the second attempt overwhelmingly did not.\n\nSome researchers argue the remaining gap is mostly attributable to self-deception rather than deliberate distortion: applicants genuinely believe they possess the traits they claim, because motivated reasoning leads them to inflate their self-assessments in positive directions. This is a different phenomenon from cynical faking, and it has different implications for what to do about it. It also connects to the broader evidence on [why self-assessment alone produces an incomplete picture](/blog/why-self-assessment-alone-isnt-enough-peer-personality-feedback): we are systematically unreliable narrators of our own personalities.\n\n## Does Faking Actually Damage Predictive Validity?\n\nThis is the key empirical question, and the answer is genuinely contested. There are three main positions in the literature:\n\n**Position 1: Faking doesn''t matter much.** The meta-analytic case here is direct. Social desirability scales were found not to predict job performance, school success, task performance or counterproductive behaviours, and social desirability did not function as a useful suppressor or mediator for job performance; removing its effects from the Big Five left their validity for predicting workplace performance intact [doi:10.1037/0021-9010.81.6.660](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.81.6.660). If fakers end up performing just as well on the job, the faking has not corrupted the predictive signal.\n\n**Position 2: Faking reduces validity.** Other research argues that when high scorers are a mixture of genuinely high-trait individuals and skilled fakers, the predictive relationship with performance is attenuated. The fakers inflate the distribution without having the underlying trait.\n\n**Position 3: Faking selects for impression management.** A third perspective notes that the ability to fake a personality test successfully may itself be a meaningful signal, specifically, it correlates with social intelligence and the motivation to impress. People who fake effectively may actually perform well in roles where impression management matters. This does not make faking unproblematic, but it complicates the simple validity-corruption story.\n\nThe current scientific consensus leans toward Position 1 for low-stakes, non-selection uses, and toward Position 2 for high-stakes selection contexts where the incentive to fake is highest. For what \"valid\" actually means in personality measurement, see [what is reliability and validity in personality testing?](/blog/what-is-reliability-validity-in-personality-testing).\n\n## Forced-Choice Formats: A Partial Solution to Test Faking\n\nOne technical response to faking is the forced-choice format, in which respondents choose between two or more equally socially desirable options (e.g., \"I tend to be very organised\" vs. \"I tend to be very sociable\"). By removing the easy option of endorsing all positive descriptors, forced-choice formats reduce the opportunity for response inflation.\n\nThe evidence is good, and it is quantified. A meta-analysis comparing forced-choice scores between low-stakes and high-stakes conditions put the overall score inflation for forced-choice measures at d = 0.06, far below the equivalent figure for single-statement measures across most facets, and identified which design choices matter: statements balanced for social desirability, normative rather than purely ipsative scoring, and multidimensional blocks [doi:10.1037/apl0000414](https://doi.org/10.1037/apl0000414). A second meta-analysis reached a compatible conclusion, that forced-choice inventories resist faking, that the effect is larger in experimental settings than in real selection, and that quasi-ipsative formats resist best, with conscientiousness inflation of δ = 0.49 under a quasi-ipsative format against δ = 1.27 under a fully ipsative one [doi:10.3389/fpsyg.2021.732241](https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.732241).\n\nThe cost is real too. Forced-choice items are harder to take, more cognitively demanding, and less well-validated in cross-cultural contexts. Purely ipsative scoring makes scores relative to each other rather than absolute, which complicates interpretation, and the second meta-analysis above shows the format choice inside \"forced-choice\" matters as much as the decision to use it. For a detailed treatment of the tradeoffs, see [forced-choice personality assessment: more honest data](/blog/forced-choice-personality-assessment-more-honest-data).\n\n| Test format | Susceptibility to faking | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| Standard Likert-scale Big Five | High, easy to endorse positive items | Use for development; monitor if used in selection |\n| Forced-choice (e.g., Thurstonian IRT) | Low overall (d = 0.06), but depends on how it is built | Better for selection contexts; validate locally |\n| Situational judgement tests (SJTs) | Moderate, less transparent but still fakeable | Combine with personality data for better coverage |\n| Peer/multi-rater assessment (e.g., Cèrcol Witnesses) | Low, others are harder to game | Most resistant to intentional faking |\n| Anonymous self-report (no high-stakes context) | Low, reduced motivation to distort | Best for development; the ideal context for honest data |\n\nSee also: [social desirability bias in personality tests](/blog/social-desirability-bias-personality-tests).\n\n## Why the Real Problem Is Using Personality Tests for Selection\n\nMost of the faking debate is actually a proxy debate about whether personality tests should be used for hiring selection at all. If you use personality data for development, to help people understand themselves and work better with others, faking is largely self-defeating. If you fake your way to a development report that doesn''t reflect how you actually behave, you will simply get feedback that is less useful to you. The cost falls on the person who faked, not on the organisation.\n\nIf you use personality data as a selection hurdle, as a gate that determines whether you proceed in a hiring process, the incentive to fake becomes significant, and the consequences of faking fall on the organisation (in the form of mis-selected hires) and on honest candidates who are disadvantaged relative to skilled fakers. The ethical and legal dimensions of this are examined in [personality testing in hiring: what is legal and what is ethical](/blog/personality-testing-in-hiring-what-is-legal-what-is-ethical).\n\nThis is the most important practical insight from the faking literature: the fakeability problem is downstream of the misuse problem. Personality assessment was designed for development and self-understanding. When it is repurposed as a selection screen, it develops fakeability vulnerabilities that it was never designed to resist.\n\n## Why Anonymous, Developmental Assessment Resists Faking\n\nOne practical response to faking concerns is to collect personality data anonymously: that is, in contexts where the data will not be seen by hiring managers, and where no individual''s scores are linked to employment decisions. This is exactly the context in which personality assessment generates its most valid and useful signal.\n\nAnonymous assessments eliminate the primary motivation for faking. They produce data that is more accurate and more useful for development. They also eliminate the legal and ethical risks associated with using personality data in selection.\n\nCèrcol uses a multi-rater design in which Witnesses provide an external view of a person''s behaviour. This adds a layer of information that is substantially harder to fake than self-report: while you can inflate your own scores on Conscientiousness, you cannot easily manipulate how five colleagues who work with you every day describe your typical behaviour. The science behind the [IPIP: International Personality Item Pool](https://ipip.ori.org) that underpins the Witness instrument gives it validated, open-source foundations that commercial tools frequently lack.\n\nThe faking literature ultimately teaches us less about how to make personality tests more tamper-resistant and more about where they genuinely belong: in development, in team dialogue, in self-understanding. That is where they deliver their most honest and most useful signal.\n\n## How Cèrcol addresses faking\n\nCèrcol''s design responds directly to the faking problem at every level. The Witness peer assessment means that your personality profile is not constructed from your own self-presentation: it is built from how the people who work with you actually describe your behaviour. Witnesses answer forced-choice adjective pairs anonymously, which removes both the ability to fake (no obviously \"correct\" answer) and the incentive (no stakes attached to their individual responses). The self-report component is taken in a low-stakes, individually owned context, you own your data and decide who sees it, which research shows substantially reduces impression management. You can explore the full instrument at [/instruments](/instruments) and read about the underlying science at [/science](/science). The assessment is free at [cercol.team](https://cercol.team).\n\n## Sources\n\n- Viswesvaran, C., & Ones, D. S. (1999). Meta-analyses of fakability estimates: Implications for personality measurement. *Educational and Psychological Measurement.* [https://doi.org/10.1177/00131649921969802](https://doi.org/10.1177/00131649921969802)\n- Birkeland, S. A., Manson, T. M., Kisamore, J. L., Brannick, M. T., & Smith, M. A. (2006). A meta-analytic investigation of job applicant faking on personality measures. *International Journal of Selection and Assessment.* [https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2389.2006.00354.x)\n- Hogan, J., Barrett, P., & Hogan, R. (2007). Personality measurement, faking, and employment selection. *Journal of Applied Psychology.* [https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.92.5.1270](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.92.5.1270)\n- Ones, D. S., Viswesvaran, C., & Reiss, A. D. (1996). Role of social desirability in personality testing for personnel selection: The red herring. *Journal of Applied Psychology.* [https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.81.6.660](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.81.6.660)\n- Cao, M., & Drasgow, F. (2019). Does forcing reduce faking? A meta-analytic review of forced-choice personality measures in high-stakes situations. *Journal of Applied Psychology.* [https://doi.org/10.1037/apl0000414](https://doi.org/10.1037/apl0000414)\n- Martínez, A., & Salgado, J. F. (2021). A meta-analysis of the faking resistance of forced-choice personality inventories. *Frontiers in Psychology.* [https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.732241](https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.732241)\n- Wikipedia: [Social desirability bias](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_desirability_bias)\n- IPIP: International Personality Item Pool: [https://ipip.ori.org](https://ipip.ori.org)\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [Social desirability bias in personality tests](/blog/social-desirability-bias-personality-tests)\n- [Forced-choice personality assessment: getting more honest data](/blog/forced-choice-personality-assessment-more-honest-data)\n- [Why self-assessment alone isn''t enough: the case for peer personality feedback](/blog/why-self-assessment-alone-isnt-enough-peer-personality-feedback)\n- [Anonymity in personality assessment: why it matters](/blog/anonymity-in-personality-assessment-why-it-matters)\n- [Personality testing in hiring: what is legal and what is ethical?](/blog/personality-testing-in-hiring-what-is-legal-what-is-ethical)\n- [What is reliability and validity in personality testing?](/blog/what-is-reliability-validity-in-personality-testing)"'::jsonb),
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE slug = 'can-you-fake-a-personality-test';
UPDATE blog_posts SET content = jsonb_set(content, '{en}', '"# Conflict resolution styles and personality: the evidence\n\nConflict is inevitable in teams that do real work. The question is not whether it will arise but how people handle it when it does, and whether their handling of it makes things better or worse. The answer varies substantially by personality.\n\nThe Thomas-Kilmann Conflict Mode Instrument (TKI), developed by Kenneth Thomas and Ralph Kilmann in the 1970s, remains the most widely used framework for classifying conflict resolution behaviour. It identifies five distinct styles along two axes: concern for one''s own goals (assertiveness) and concern for the other party''s goals (cooperativeness). Each style reflects a different trade-off between these values. And each maps, with notable consistency, to specific Big Five personality profiles.\n\nUnderstanding which conflict style a person defaults to, and why, is not about labelling or limiting people. It is about understanding the automatic tendencies that surface under pressure, before deliberate choice kicks in.\n\n---\n\n## The Five Thomas-Kilmann Conflict Styles Explained\n\nA useful overview of the conflict resolution literature is available at [Wikipedia: Conflict resolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conflict_resolution). The [Thomas-Kilmann Conflict Mode Instrument](https://kilmanndiagnostics.com/overview-thomas-kilmann-conflict-mode-instrument-tki/) remains the standard tool for assessing these styles in organisational settings.\n\n**Competing** is high-assertiveness, low-cooperativeness. The competing style treats conflict as a contest to be won. The individual pursues their own goals at the expense of the other party''s, uses positional power where available, and resists compromise that would reduce their outcome.\n\n**Collaborating** is high-assertiveness, high-cooperativeness. The collaborating style treats conflict as a shared problem to be solved. Both parties'' interests are taken seriously, creative solutions are sought, and the relationship is maintained throughout.\n\n**Compromising** is moderate on both axes. Neither party gets what they fully wanted, but both get something. It is faster than collaborating but less likely to produce genuinely creative outcomes.\n\n**Avoiding** is low-assertiveness, low-cooperativeness. The avoiding style withdraws from conflict, defers the issue, or denies that a conflict exists. It is the style of hoping the problem resolves itself.\n\n**Accommodating** is low-assertiveness, high-cooperativeness. The accommodating style concedes to the other party''s position, prioritising the relationship over the outcome. It preserves harmony at the cost of advocacy.\n\nThe research literature uses a slightly different vocabulary for the same five modes, following Rahim''s inventory: integrating for collaborating, obliging for accommodating, dominating for competing, and avoiding and compromising as above. The studies cited below use those names.\n\n---\n\n## How Each Big Five Dimension Predicts Your Conflict Style Default\n\nResearch mapping Big Five personality to conflict styles converges on a consistent set of associations. The most comprehensive single study remains Antonioni (1998) [doi:10.1108/eb022814](https://doi.org/10.1108/eb022814), who surveyed 351 students and, as a check on generalising beyond students, 110 managers. The pattern has since been pooled meta-analytically by Tehrani and Yamini (2020) [doi:10.1016/j.paid.2019.109794](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.paid.2019.109794) across 20 studies and 5,337 participants.\n\nWhat replicates in this literature is the direction of each association, not its magnitude. Point estimates for any given trait-and-style pair move considerably from sample to sample, and neither Antonioni nor the meta-analysis supports a single headline coefficient for, say, Agreeableness and collaborating. The sections below therefore describe directions, which hold up, and avoid coefficients, which do not.\n\n### Agreeableness (Bond) → accommodating and avoiding\n\nThis is the most robust finding in the literature. Antonioni (1998) found Agreeableness positively related to both the obliging and the avoiding styles, and Tehrani and Yamini (2020) report the same two associations across their pooled samples. Individuals high in Agreeableness, high Bond in Cèrcol''s framework, show a strong preference for accommodating and, to a lesser extent, avoiding. The underlying mechanism is clear: high-Bond individuals place high value on interpersonal harmony and experience conflict as genuinely aversive, not merely inconvenient. They are motivated to resolve the discomfort of conflict quickly, and the fastest route to resolution is to give the other party what they want.\n\nGraziano, Jensen-Campbell and Hair (1996) [doi:10.1037/0022-3514.70.4.820](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.70.4.820) supply the behavioural detail. Across five different kinds of interpersonal relationship, participants at both ends of the Agreeableness scale rated negotiation and disengagement as better tactics than power assertion, but participants low in Agreeableness rated power assertion as a better choice than high-agreeable participants did. The difference is not that agreeable people prefer harmony and disagreeable people prefer war; it is that power assertion stays on the menu for one group and comes off it for the other.\n\nThis is not pathological. Accommodation is often the right call, in contexts where the relationship matters more than the specific outcome, or where the other party''s position is more informed. The risk arises when high-Bond individuals accommodate habitually, not selectively. Teams where everyone scores high on Bond can slide toward [groupthink and an inability to surface honest disagreement](/blog/too-agreeable-why-high-bond-teams-struggle-with-honest-feedback), a phenomenon worth understanding alongside conflict style.\n\n### Conscientiousness (Discipline) → collaborating, not avoiding\n\nHigh-Discipline individuals engage with conflict rather than sidestep it. Antonioni (1998) found Conscientiousness positively related to the integrating style and negatively related to avoiding, and Tehrani and Yamini (2020) report the same directions. Goal-directedness and self-regulatory capacity make the work of collaborating affordable: collaborating is more effort than the alternatives, and high-Discipline people are willing to spend it.\n\nThe trait that predicts both integrating and dominating is Extraversion, not Conscientiousness. Antonioni found Extraversion positively related to integrating and positively related to dominating, which is why a high-Presence colleague can look collaborative and combative within the same meeting. The [Vision-Discipline tension in teams](/blog/the-vision-discipline-tension-innovation-vs-execution) explores how this plays out when high-Discipline members clash with exploratory, Vision-oriented colleagues.\n\n### Openness to Experience (Vision) → collaborating\n\nHigh-Vision individuals are consistent adopters of the collaborating style. Antonioni (1998) found Openness positively related to integrating and negatively related to avoiding, and Tehrani and Yamini (2020) report the same directions in their pooled data. Collaborating requires intellectual flexibility, genuine curiosity about the other party''s perspective, and comfort with the ambiguity that arises when two people''s interests do not obviously align. These are precisely the cognitive and motivational qualities associated with high Openness.\n\n### Neuroticism (Depth) → avoiding\n\nHigh Depth, high Neuroticism, is a reliable predictor of conflict avoidance. Antonioni (1998) found Neuroticism positively related to avoiding and negatively related to dominating, and the meta-analytic pooling in Tehrani and Yamini (2020) confirms the positive Neuroticism-avoiding association. The threat sensitivity associated with high Neuroticism makes conflict feel particularly dangerous, not just uncomfortable. For individuals high in Depth, direct confrontation activates anxiety responses that make avoidance feel genuinely protective rather than merely expedient. Avoiding removes the threat stimulus.\n\nNote that Depth is not alone here. Bond predicts avoidance too, for entirely different reasons, and in the pooled data both associations are present. A team can under-surface disagreement because people are anxious, because people are kind, or both, and the fix is not the same in the two cases.\n\nThe cost is that avoided conflicts rarely resolve. They accumulate. For high-Depth team members especially, the fear that confrontation will make things worse is real, but so is the evidence that non-confrontation tends to make things worse on a longer timeline. This dynamic is explored in depth in [what personality conflict in teams actually looks like](/blog/personality-conflict-in-teams-what-it-actually-looks-like).\n\n### Extraversion (Presence) → competing\n\nHigh Presence individuals are more likely to use the competing style. Antonioni (1998) found Extraversion positively related to the dominating style, and negatively related to avoiding. Their comfort with social assertiveness, preference for direct communication, and higher tolerance for interpersonal friction reduce the inhibition cost of taking a strong position publicly.\n\n---\n\n## Which Conflict Resolution Style Produces the Best Team Outcomes?\n\nThis is where the literature is thinner than the management writing around it suggests, and where a claim that gets repeated constantly does not survive contact with its source.\n\nThe most cited meta-analysis in this area, De Dreu and Weingart (2003) [doi:10.1037/0021-9010.88.4.741](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.88.4.741), is routinely invoked to show that collaborating beats the alternatives. It shows no such thing, because it did not measure conflict styles at all. What it measured was how much conflict of each type a team had. Relationship conflict correlated negatively with team performance (ρ = −.22). Task conflict, contrary to the then-standard view that disagreement about the work is productive, correlated negatively too (ρ = −.23). Conflict of both kinds was more damaging on complex decision-making and project tasks than on routine production tasks. No correlation in that analysis came out positive.\n\nThe claim that how a team argues matters more than how much it argues does have meta-analytic support, but from a different paper. DeChurch, Mesmer-Magnus and Doty (2013) [doi:10.1037/a0032896](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0032896) separated conflict states, meaning the source and intensity of perceived incompatibility, from conflict processes, meaning how teams actually behave while disagreeing, across 45 independent studies and 3,218 teams. Controlling for conflict states, conflict processes explained an additional 13% of the variance in both team performance and team affective outcomes, and the authors identify some processes as beneficial to teams and others as detrimental.\n\nThat is the defensible version of the argument for collaborating. Not that a named style has a known effect size on team performance, because nobody has established that, but that the behaviour a team chooses during disagreement carries weight independent of how much disagreement there is. Choosing it well is worth doing on that basis alone.\n\nThe important caveat is that collaborating is cognitively and emotionally expensive. It takes longer, requires greater tolerance for ambiguity, and demands that both parties bring the interpersonal skill and motivation to engage honestly. Teams under high time pressure, or teams where trust is low, may find compromising the practical optimum even if it is theoretically second-best. [Building trust in teams](/blog/trust-in-teams-personality-foundations) is therefore a prerequisite for making the collaborating style consistently available.\n\n---\n\n## When Mixed Conflict Styles Create a Meta-Conflict of Their Own\n\nA team that contains one high-Bond accommodator, one high-Presence competitor, one high-Depth avoider, and one high-Vision collaborator is not better off just because of its diversity. Without explicit awareness of how those styles interact, the diversity becomes a source of confusion and frustration.\n\nThe competitor sees the accommodator as weak and the avoider as passive-aggressive. The accommodator experiences the competitor as aggressive and finds the collaborator exhausting (too much process before resolution). The avoider feels overwhelmed by the competitor and unsafe with the collaborator''s directness. The collaborator feels unsupported by the avoider and frustrated by the competitor''s refusal to explore creative solutions.\n\nThis is not a hypothetical scenario. It is what happens in many teams every time significant disagreement arises. The conflict style mismatch itself becomes a conflict. [How to give personality-informed feedback](/blog/how-to-give-personality-informed-feedback) offers practical tools for navigating these conversations productively.\n\nThe solution is not personality homogeneity, diversity in conflict style is genuinely useful in teams that can use it. The solution is explicit, pre-agreed team conflict protocols: how will we handle it when we disagree? What do we do when someone is avoiding? What does it mean when someone competes hard? Building these agreements before conflict arises, when no one''s interests are yet at stake, is considerably more effective than improvising under pressure. It is also the part of this article with the strongest empirical backing, since it is the conflict process, not the amount of conflict, that DeChurch and colleagues found to carry independent variance.\n\n---\n\n## Conflict Styles, Personality Profiles, and When Each Works\n\n| Conflict style | Personality profile | When it works | When it fails |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Competing | High Presence | Emergencies; clear expertise advantage; adversarial negotiations | Destroys trust; shuts down collaborative problem-solving; escalates relationship conflict |\n| Collaborating | High Vision + high Discipline, with Presence and Bond also positively associated | Complex, creative problems; high-trust contexts; long-term relationships | Too slow under time pressure; requires both parties'' willingness and skill |\n| Compromising | Positively associated with Bond, Presence, Vision and Discipline | Time-constrained settings; when good enough is genuinely enough | Leaves creative solutions undiscovered; can feel like loss to both parties |\n| Avoiding | High Depth or high Bond, low Presence | When the issue is genuinely trivial; cooling-off periods before re-engagement | Conflicts accumulate; unresolved tensions corrode team functioning |\n| Accommodating | High Bond | When the relationship outweighs the outcome; when the other party has superior information | Habitual accommodation signals that honesty is not valued; high-Bond members stop advocating |\n\nThe profile column reports the directions found in Antonioni (1998) and Tehrani and Yamini (2020), not effect sizes. Read it as which traits tilt a person toward a style, not as a prediction about any individual.\n\n---\n\n## Using Team Personality Data to Design Better Conflict Protocols\n\nCèrcol''s Witness-based personality assessments provide teams with a composition map that makes conflict style tendencies visible before the conflict arrives. A team that can see that four of its five members score high on Bond and low on Depth has predictive information: this team is likely to under-surface disagreement and over-accommodate. Designing against that tendency, by creating explicit norms for voicing dissent, using anonymous pre-deliberation input, or rotating a designated challenger role, becomes a concrete and evidence-grounded act rather than a vague aspiration.\n\nUnderstanding conflict style is not about predicting that someone will always compete or always avoid. It is about understanding the default setting, the response that arises before reflection kicks in. With that knowledge, teams can design better, not just hope for better.\n\n---\n\n## See How Conflict Styles Show Up on Your Team\n\nUnderstanding conflict style tendencies in the abstract is one thing; seeing them mapped to your actual team is another. Cèrcol''s [Witness assessment](https://cercol.team) collects peer personality data across all five dimensions, including the Bond and Depth scores that most directly predict conflict avoidance and accommodation patterns. The [Witness instrument](/instruments) is specifically designed to surface gaps between self-perception and how teammates actually experience someone, which is precisely where conflict-style blind spots tend to live. If your team regularly experiences friction without resolution, or consensus without genuine agreement, the personality composition data is often the most informative place to start. You can run a free assessment at [cercol.team](https://cercol.team) and explore the full [12-role framework](/roles) that shows how trait combinations translate into team function.\n\n---\n\n*Further reading: [What personality conflict in teams actually looks like](/blog/personality-conflict-in-teams-what-it-actually-looks-like) · [How to give personality-informed feedback](/blog/how-to-give-personality-informed-feedback)*\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [What Is Agreeableness](/blog/what-is-agreeableness-the-cooperative-dimension)\n- [Personality Conflict in Teams: What It Actually Looks Like](/blog/personality-conflict-in-teams-what-it-actually-looks-like)\n- [Building Psychological Safety: Personality Science](/blog/building-psychological-safety-personality-science)\n- [Trust in Teams: Personality Foundations](/blog/trust-in-teams-personality-foundations)\n- [Too Agreeable? Why High-Bond Teams Struggle with Honest Feedback](/blog/too-agreeable-why-high-bond-teams-struggle-with-honest-feedback)\n- [How to Give Personality-Informed Feedback](/blog/how-to-give-personality-informed-feedback)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Antonioni, D. (1998). Relationship between the Big Five personality factors and conflict management styles. *International Journal of Conflict Management*, 9(4), 336–355. [doi:10.1108/eb022814](https://doi.org/10.1108/eb022814)\n- De Dreu, C. K. W., & Weingart, L. R. (2003). Task versus relationship conflict, team performance, and team member satisfaction: A meta-analysis. *Journal of Applied Psychology*, 88(4), 741–749. [doi:10.1037/0021-9010.88.4.741](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.88.4.741)\n- DeChurch, L. A., Mesmer-Magnus, J. R., & Doty, D. (2013). Moving beyond relationship and task conflict: Toward a process-state perspective. *Journal of Applied Psychology*, 98(4), 559–578. [doi:10.1037/a0032896](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0032896)\n- Graziano, W. G., Jensen-Campbell, L. A., & Hair, E. C. (1996). Perceiving interpersonal conflict and reacting to it: The case for agreeableness. *Journal of Personality and Social Psychology*, 70(4), 820–835. [doi:10.1037/0022-3514.70.4.820](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.70.4.820)\n- Tehrani, H. D., & Yamini, S. (2020). Personality traits and conflict resolution styles: A meta-analysis. *Personality and Individual Differences*, 157, 109794. [doi:10.1016/j.paid.2019.109794](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.paid.2019.109794)\n"'::jsonb),
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE slug = 'conflict-resolution-styles-personality';
UPDATE blog_posts SET content = jsonb_set(content, '{en}', '"# Do generational differences in personality exist?\n\nOpen any HR conference programme and you will find sessions on managing Millennials, understanding Gen Z, or bridging the gap between Boomers and younger cohorts. The generational framework has become the dominant lens through which organisations interpret differences in values, work styles, and expectations. It has also become, in the view of most personality researchers, one of the most thoroughly misunderstood frameworks in applied psychology.\n\nThis article examines what the longitudinal data actually shows about generational differences in personality, and why the popular generational narrative deserves serious scepticism.\n\n---\n\n\n<div class=\"stat-grid\">\n  <div class=\"stat-card\">\n    <div class=\"stat-value\">δ = 0.15</div>\n    <div class=\"stat-label\">average generational difference across 16 work outcomes (negligible)</div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"stat-card\">\n    <div class=\"stat-value\">r = 0.64</div>\n    <div class=\"stat-label\">test-retest consistency of personality traits at age 30</div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"stat-card\" style=\"border-top: 3px solid #cf3339;\">\n    <div class=\"stat-value\" style=\"color:#cf3339;\">Myth</div>\n    <div class=\"stat-label\">''Millennials are more narcissistic'', not supported by data</div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\nThose three figures come from three places, and it is worth being precise about each. The 0.15 is the mean corrected standardised difference (δ) that Ravid, Costanza and Romero (2025) [doi:10.1002/job.2827](https://doi.org/10.1002/job.2827) found comparing Generation X with Baby Boomers across sixteen work outcomes; their meta-analysis set ±0.15 in advance as the boundary for a practically negligible effect, and the observed average sat on it. The 0.64 is the meta-analytic test-retest correlation for personality traits at age 30 over a 6.7-year interval, from Roberts and DelVecchio''s review of 152 longitudinal studies [doi:10.1037/0033-2909.126.1.3](https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.126.1.3). The narcissism claim is dealt with in full below. Note the shape of the evidence: personality is measurably stable inside a person over decades, while the gap between birth cohorts is small enough that researchers have to run equivalence tests to describe it.\n\n## Where the Millennial vs Boomer Personality Stereotype Comes From\n\nThe popular account runs roughly as follows. Baby Boomers (born roughly 1946–1964) are disciplined, loyal to institutions, and resistant to change, rigid in the pejorative sense. Millennials (born roughly 1981–1996) are entitled, purpose-driven, and technologically fluent but emotionally fragile. [Gen Z (born roughly 1997–2012)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generation_Z) are anxious, politically engaged, digitally native, and sceptical of traditional authority. Gen X floats in the middle, perpetually overlooked.\n\nThese characterisations circulate constantly in management writing, generational consultancy products, and media commentary. They have the ring of empirical claims. They are, with very few exceptions, poorly supported by rigorous evidence.\n\nThe framing gets the ontology wrong before it gets the numbers wrong. Generations are not personality types. They are administrative categories defined by birth year, a serviceable sociological tool for some purposes and a poor one for predicting individual psychology. The variation inside any one cohort on any one personality dimension is far larger than the gap between cohort averages, which is why cohort membership tells you so little about the person in front of you.\n\n---\n\n## Why Generation Is Not a Valid Personality Type in Big Five Research\n\nBefore examining what longitudinal data shows, it is worth understanding why the generational framework is methodologically suspect for personality research.\n\n**The base rate problem.** A generation spans roughly 15–20 birth years. Within any such cohort there is enormous variation in every personality dimension. A Millennial born in 1981 who grew up in rural poverty, entered the workforce in 2003, and experienced the 2008 financial crisis in their late twenties has a fundamentally different formative context than a Millennial born in 1995 who grew up in an urban professional household and graduated in 2017. Lumping them into the same \"personality type\" is a category error.\n\n**Confounding with age effects.** People change across the lifespan. [Conscientiousness (Discipline) tends to increase with age; Neuroticism (Depth) tends to decrease](/blog/do-personality-traits-change-over-a-lifetime). Any study that compares 25-year-olds to 55-year-olds is partly measuring personality development, not generational difference. Disentangling cohort effects from age effects requires longitudinal designs that track the same individuals over time, and most generational claims are not based on this kind of data.\n\n**Confounding with period effects.** Economic recessions, pandemics, technological disruptions, and social movements affect everyone alive at a given time, not just one cohort. The anxiety documented in young people during and after COVID-19 is not a generational personality trait: it is a period effect that will likely moderate as conditions change.\n\n**Measurement non-equivalence.** If a questionnaire item means something subtly different to a student in 1992 and a student in 2012, a cohort comparison of scores on that item is comparing two different things. This sounds like a technicality. It is not, as the narcissism case below demonstrates.\n\n---\n\n## What Longitudinal Big Five Data Actually Shows About Generations\n\nThe most rigorous work on cohort and generational differences comes from research that uses large, representative samples and pools across studies. The picture that emerges is more modest than the popular narrative.\n\n**The workplace differences are small to nonexistent.** Costanza and colleagues (2012) [doi:10.1007/s10869-012-9259-4](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10869-012-9259-4) meta-analysed 20 studies covering 19,961 subjects and 18 pairwise generational comparisons on job satisfaction, organisational commitment and intent to turnover. Corrected mean differences ranged from .02 to .25 for job satisfaction, −.22 to .46 for organisational commitment, and −.62 to .05 for intent to turnover, a pattern the authors described as moderate to small and essentially zero in many cases. Their conclusion was that meaningful generational differences probably do not exist on those variables and that whatever differences appear are likely attributable to something other than generational membership.\n\n**A larger and more recent pooling reached the same place.** Ravid, Costanza and Romero (2025) repeated the exercise with more primary studies and formal equivalence testing, and again found few systematic, meaningful differences among generations across a range of outcomes. The general absence of differences in work values is particularly awkward for the generational literature, since those purported differences are central to most generational models.\n\n**Personality is stable inside individuals.** Roberts and DelVecchio (2000) compiled 3,217 test-retest correlations from 152 longitudinal studies. Trait consistency rose from .31 in childhood to .54 during the college years, .64 at age 30, and a plateau around .74 between ages 50 and 70, with the interval held constant at 6.7 years. Personality is not fixed, but a person''s standing relative to their peers is considerably more durable than their birth-year cohort is informative. For more on why [Conscientiousness is the most consistent predictor of job performance](/blog/what-is-conscientiousness-the-most-consistent-predictor-of-job-performance), see the dedicated explainer.\n\n**The narcissism claim went the other way.** Jean Twenge''s analyses of Narcissistic Personality Inventory scores across university student cohorts reported incremental increases, and that work is the origin of nearly every \"Millennials are more narcissistic\" headline. Wetzel and colleagues (2017) [doi:10.1177/0956797617724208](https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797617724208) then ran the check the earlier analyses had skipped: whether the NPI measures the same construct in different cohorts. Several items did not. Modelling that non-equivalence, across student cohorts of 1,166 (1990s), 33,647 (2000s) and 25,412 (2010s), overall narcissism showed a small *decline* from the 1990s to the 2010s (d = −0.27), with the leadership (d = −0.20), vanity (d = −0.16) and entitlement (d = −0.28) facets all falling. Brent Roberts, a longstanding methodological critic of the generational narcissism literature, is a co-author. This is why the third card above says myth rather than contested: the best-controlled test of the claim found the opposite of the claim. The [personality science replication crisis](/blog/personality-science-replication-crisis) is the wider context for how a finding like this circulates for a decade before anyone checks the measurement assumptions.\n\nThe current evidence therefore suggests: cohort differences that are small relative to within-cohort variance; personality that is substantially stable within individuals across the lifespan; and a considerable gap between what the data shows and how generational claims are presented in public discourse.\n\n---\n\n## Why the Generational Personality Frame Is Largely a Media Construct\n\nThe generational personality narrative persists because it is commercially and rhetorically useful, not because it is scientifically accurate.\n\nIt is commercially useful because it creates a market for generational consultancy, management training programmes, and books that promise to decode the mysterious Millennial employee. Simplifying complex social change into personality types that can be packaged and sold is a reliable business model.\n\nIt is rhetorically useful because it provides a ready-made explanation for workplace tensions that would otherwise require structural analysis. \"Millennials don''t respect hierarchy\" is easier to manage than \"our hierarchical structures are inefficient and alienate people who have been socialised to expect transparency and meritocracy.\" Attributing conflict to personality cohorts shifts attention away from institutional design.\n\n| Generational stereotype | What the evidence actually shows | What actually explains it |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"Millennials are entitled\" | No robust Big Five evidence for cohort-level sense of entitlement. Once measurement equivalence is modelled, the entitlement facet of the NPI has fallen across cohorts, not risen (Wetzel et al., 2017). | Entry into a labour market with worse conditions than previous cohorts, despite higher credential requirements. Frustration is rational, not dispositional. |\n| \"Gen Z is anxious\" | Higher self-reported anxiety in young people in post-2015 data. Likely period effect (social media, economic uncertainty, COVID-19) rather than stable personality cohort difference. | Period-specific stressors affecting all young people in that historical window, not a generational personality type. |\n| \"Boomers are rigid\" | Conscientiousness and Openness show age-related but not cohort-specific effects in the predicted direction. Older adults in any cohort show somewhat lower Openness on average. | Normal lifespan personality development, not generational character. Will apply to Millennials as they age. |\n| \"Gen X is sceptical\" | No consistent Big Five evidence for cohort-level scepticism or cynicism. Costanza et al. (2012) found generational differences in commitment and turnover intent to be small or essentially zero. | Economic and political context of late Cold War and early deindustrialisation. A period effect, not a personality endowment. |\n\n---\n\n## What Actually Predicts Generational Differences in Workplace Behaviour\n\nIf generational personality differences are mostly noise, what actually explains the real differences in work values and expectations that practitioners observe?\n\n**Economic context at career entry.** The research on \"cohort scarring\" shows that people who enter the labour market during recessions develop persistently different attitudes toward job security, institutional loyalty, and risk, not because their personalities are different, but because their economic experience at a critical developmental moment was different. This looks like a personality difference but is better understood as a rational adaptive response to structural conditions.\n\n**Technological socialisation.** People who grew up with ubiquitous smartphones and social media have different communication habits and expectations than those who did not. This is a behavioural and attitudinal difference, not a Big Five personality difference. It is also largely generalisable within any sufficiently technology-exposed cohort regardless of birth year.\n\n**Educational norms.** The shift toward more participatory, less authoritarian educational styles that occurred in many Western countries from the 1980s onward affects people who went through those educational systems, and their expectations of appropriate workplace relationships. Again, this is not a personality cohort effect; it is a socialisation effect that will evolve as educational norms continue to change.\n\n---\n\n## What Personality Science Means for Managing Across Generations\n\nThe practical implication is not that every difference between age groups is fictional. It is that using generational categories as a proxy for personality profiling is a significant methodological error, with real costs.\n\nDesigning onboarding programmes, feedback structures, or management approaches based on assumed generational personalities will systematically misread individuals. The Millennial employee who values structured feedback and long-term institutional commitment will be poorly served by a framework that assumes their birth year makes them allergic to hierarchy. The Boomer employee who is highly open to change and hungry for novel challenges will be invisible in a framework that treats their generation as inherently resistant to it. Costanza and colleagues drew the operational conclusion themselves: targeted organisational interventions addressing generational differences may not be effective, because there is not much there to target.\n\nThe same caution applies to [cross-cultural personality generalisations](/blog/big-five-personality-across-cultures-what-research-shows): group averages are a poor substitute for individual measurement. For more on how personality changes over time, see [do personality traits change over a lifetime](/blog/do-personality-traits-change-over-a-lifetime). For the broader question of what personality science can and cannot predict, see [personality science: limits and what it cannot predict](/blog/personality-science-limits-what-it-cannot-predict).\n\n---\n\n## Stop Profiling the Birth Year, Start Measuring the Person\n\nGenerational frameworks are a shortcut, not a science. They replace individual measurement with demographic assumption, and in doing so they misread the very people they claim to explain. Birth year tells you almost nothing about where someone sits on Discipline, Depth, Presence, Bond, or Vision, the dimensions that actually predict how they work and collaborate. Cèrcol''s free Big Five assessment takes about fifteen minutes and gives you real scores for the real person in front of you, not a cohort stereotype. If you want to understand your team, start by measuring each member as an individual rather than a generation. [Take the free assessment at cercol.team](https://cercol.team).\n\n---\n\n*Further reading: [Generation Z, Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generation_Z) · [Millennials, Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millennials)*\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [Do Personality Traits Change Over a Lifetime](/blog/do-personality-traits-change-over-a-lifetime)\n- [Big Five Personality Across Cultures: What Research Shows](/blog/big-five-personality-across-cultures-what-research-shows)\n- [Gender and Personality: What Big Five Research Says](/blog/gender-and-personality-what-big-five-research-says)\n- [Five Personality Science Myths That Won''t Die](/blog/five-personality-science-myths-that-wont-die)\n- [Personality Science: Replication Crisis](/blog/personality-science-replication-crisis)\n- [Can Personality Be Changed](/blog/can-personality-be-changed-coaching-therapy-evidence)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Costanza, D. P., Badger, J. M., Fraser, R. L., Severt, J. B., & Gade, P. A. (2012). Generational differences in work-related attitudes: A meta-analysis. *Journal of Business and Psychology*, 27(4), 375–394. [doi:10.1007/s10869-012-9259-4](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10869-012-9259-4)\n- Ravid, D. M., Costanza, D. P., & Romero, M. R. (2025). Generational differences at work? A meta-analysis and qualitative investigation. *Journal of Organizational Behavior*, 46(1), 43–65. [doi:10.1002/job.2827](https://doi.org/10.1002/job.2827)\n- Roberts, B. W., & DelVecchio, W. F. (2000). The rank-order consistency of personality traits from childhood to old age: A quantitative review of longitudinal studies. *Psychological Bulletin*, 126(1), 3–25. [doi:10.1037/0033-2909.126.1.3](https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.126.1.3)\n- Wetzel, E., Brown, A., Hill, P. L., Chung, J. M., Robins, R. W., & Roberts, B. W. (2017). The narcissism epidemic is dead; long live the narcissism epidemic. *Psychological Science*, 28(12), 1833–1847. [doi:10.1177/0956797617724208](https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797617724208)\n"'::jsonb),
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE slug = 'do-generational-differences-in-personality-actually-exist';
UPDATE blog_posts SET content = jsonb_set(content, '{en}', '"# Why some people reject feedback: the Big Five explanation\n\nGiving feedback is easy. Giving feedback that actually changes behaviour is not. The gap between the two is larger than most managers appreciate, and a significant portion of that gap is explained by personality.\n\nHow large the gap is has been measured. Kluger and DeNisi''s meta-analysis (1996) [doi:10.1037/0033-2909.119.2.254](https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.119.2.254) pooled 607 effect sizes across 23,663 observations and found that feedback interventions improved performance on average (d = 0.41), but that over a third of them made performance worse. Their explanation is that feedback moves the recipient''s attention, and that the further attention moves up from the task toward the self, the less effective the feedback becomes. That is a finding about the recipient, not about the delivery.\n\nThe research on feedback reception begins with the same observation: the same feedback, delivered in the same way, produces dramatically different outcomes depending on who receives it. Some people integrate critical feedback readily, use it to recalibrate, and show visible behavioural change. Others become defensive, dismiss the feedback, or appear to accept it while continuing exactly as before. These are not character flaws or failures of professionalism: they are expressions of underlying differences that interact with the structure and content of feedback in systematic ways.\n\nUnderstanding those interactions is not a soft-skills nicety. It is the difference between feedback that functions as an instrument of genuine development and feedback that functions as a bureaucratic ritual that costs time and damages relationships. For the delivery side of this equation, how to adapt what you say to who you''re saying it to, see [How to give personality-informed feedback](/blog/how-to-give-personality-informed-feedback).\n\nA caution before the dimensions. The evidence base here is thinner than the confident tone of most management writing on the subject. There is good meta-analytic work on feedback interventions and on feedback-seeking behaviour, and there are solid single studies on individual traits and what people do after receiving multisource feedback. There is no meta-analytic table of Big Five correlations with feedback reception. Where this article states a finding, it names the study. Where it describes a mechanism that is plausible but unmeasured, it says so.\n\n---\n\n## The Ilgen Model: Why Feedback Reception Is a Recipient Problem\n\nThe foundational framework for treating feedback as a reception problem rather than a delivery problem comes from Ilgen, Fisher and Taylor (1979) [doi:10.1037/0021-9010.64.4.349](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.64.4.349). Their review organises the effect of feedback on behaviour around three things that happen inside the recipient: the way the feedback is perceived, whether the recipient accepts it, and whether the recipient is willing to respond to it.\n\nThat structure is the reason source and content matter at all. A message from a distrusted source fails at perception or acceptance; a message with no actionable content fails at willingness to respond. Nothing a manager does to the message can bypass those three gates, which is why two people can leave the same conversation having received, in any meaningful sense, two different pieces of feedback.\n\nTwo decades of follow-up work sharpened the recipient side further. Ashford, Blatt and VandeWalle (2003) [doi:10.1016/S0149-2063(03)00079-5](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0149-2063%2803%2900079-5) organised the research on feedback-seeking around three motives: an instrumental motive to achieve a goal, an ego-based motive to protect one''s ego, and an image-based motive to protect one''s standing in the organisation. Those motives govern how often someone seeks feedback, whether they ask directly or watch for cues, when they ask, whom they ask, and what they are willing to ask about. Two of the three are defensive, which is a useful thing to hold in mind before assuming that a quiet employee simply does not want to improve.\n\nThe relevant Wikipedia overview is at [Wikipedia: Feedback](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feedback).\n\n---\n\n## Depth (Neuroticism): Threat Sensitivity and Defensive Reception\n\nOf all the Big Five dimensions, Neuroticism (Depth in Cèrcol''s framework) is the one most often nominated as the predictor of defensive feedback reception. The proposed mechanism is not complicated: feedback, particularly critical feedback, is a social threat stimulus. Individuals high in Depth have chronically elevated threat sensitivity, their threat-detection systems activate more readily, more intensely, and are slower to return to baseline.\n\nIn feedback contexts, this would mean that high-Depth individuals experience critical assessment not just as information but as danger. The emotional response, anxiety, shame, defensive self-protection, is automatic rather than calculated. It precedes reflection. And once that response is activated, cognitive processing of the feedback content becomes significantly impaired. The person is managing the threat, not processing the information. For a full account of what this dimension involves, see [What is Neuroticism: understanding emotional depth at work](/blog/what-is-neuroticism-understanding-emotional-depth-at-work).\n\nThe closest thing to direct evidence is reported in Smither, London and Reilly''s review and meta-analysis of multisource feedback (2005) [doi:10.1111/j.1744-6570.2005.514_1.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1744-6570.2005.514_1.x): leaders low in emotional stability, which is the high-Depth end of this dimension, described themselves as angry, frustrated, unhappy, discouraged and disappointed after receiving multisource feedback, even though the feedback they actually received was no less favourable than what other leaders received. Same message, different experience. The variance is in the recipient.\n\nWhat does not exist is a coefficient. There is no meta-analytic estimate of the association between Neuroticism and defensive feedback responding, and the largest meta-analysis in the adjacent literature, Anseel and colleagues on feedback-seeking behaviour (2015) [doi:10.1177/0149206313484521](https://doi.org/10.1177/0149206313484521), does not include Big Five traits among its antecedents at all. Treat the mechanism above as a well-motivated account with one supporting observation, not as a measured effect.\n\nThe implication for feedback delivery is not that high-Depth individuals cannot receive critical feedback. It is that the delivery conditions plausibly matter more for them than for anyone else. Separating the feedback from any implicit threat to the relationship, grounding the feedback in observable behaviour rather than character, and allowing processing time before expecting a response are all cheap to do and consistent with what the dimension is.\n\n---\n\n## Discipline (Conscientiousness): Strong on Acting, Uncertain on Receiving\n\nConscientiousness is the dimension with the most direct evidence, and the evidence is about what people do after feedback rather than how they feel during it.\n\nSmither, London and Reilly (2005) report that leaders high in Conscientiousness were more likely to participate in developmental activities after a multisource feedback process, and more likely to believe their performance had improved six months later. Those high in the responsibility component specifically said they felt obligated to use the feedback. In a separate study they review, Conscientiousness was positively related to actual performance improvement, measured as enhanced effectiveness as a team member, after peer feedback collected from different peers across two semesters. Openness to experience was positively related to improvement in the same study, and emotional stability was positively related to a psychologist''s rating of a leader''s motivation to use their results.\n\nThere is a plausible counterweight that the evidence does not settle. Individuals very high in Conscientiousness typically build well-organised self-concepts around competence and reliability, and critical feedback that challenges those self-concepts may be experienced as a threat to identity rather than as data about performance. The higher the Discipline score, the more self-defining the role of \"competent, reliable person\", and the more costly the admission of having fallen short of one''s own standards.\n\nThat is a reasonable inference from what the trait is. It is not a finding. No study located for this article reports a U-shaped relationship between Conscientiousness and feedback receptivity, and the advice that follows from it, framing feedback in terms of process gaps rather than outcome failures and always supplying a remediation path, is offered as a hypothesis worth testing on your own team rather than as a measured effect.\n\n---\n\n## Presence (Extraversion): Seeking Is Documented, Absorbing Is Not\n\nExtraversion (Presence) has one documented association in this literature: Smither, London and Reilly (2005) report that leaders higher in Extraversion were more likely to request additional feedback after their multisource results arrived. That is a finding about seeking, and it is consistent with the wider feedback-seeking research, where the act of asking carries social costs that high-Presence individuals discount more readily. Understanding [what Extraversion involves beyond the introvert-extrovert binary](/blog/what-is-extraversion-beyond-the-introvert-extrovert-binary) is useful context here.\n\nWhat is often claimed alongside it, that high-Presence recipients absorb spoken feedback better than written feedback and do poorly with delayed written reviews, is not something any study located for this article measures. It fits what the dimension is: high-Presence individuals process through social interaction and their cognitive style is more verbal and more energised by interpersonal exchange. It has not been tested as a difference in absorption.\n\nThe practical recommendation survives the downgrade, because it costs nothing. Delivering feedback to a high-Presence recipient in a real-time conversation, and using the written record as a reference rather than as the delivery vehicle, is a design choice you can try and evaluate. Just do not present it as a research finding.\n\n---\n\n## Bond (Agreeableness): When Warm Agreement Means Nothing Changed\n\nHigh Agreeableness (Bond) is associated with a different and equally important failure mode in feedback conversations: acquiescence. High-Bond individuals are motivated by social harmony and tend to respond to feedback in ways that preserve the relationship, nodding, agreeing, expressing gratitude. The problem is that this response can be entirely genuine interpersonally and entirely disconnected from actual behaviour change.\n\nHigh-Bond individuals are not being dishonest when they accept feedback warmly and then continue as before. They are responding to the social meaning of the interaction, demonstrating that they value the relationship, that they respect the feedback-giver''s perspective, that they are not defensive or difficult. The content of the feedback may or may not have been cognitively processed and internalised. [Personality and communication style](/blog/personality-and-communication-style-direct-vs-diplomatic) research helps explain why high-Bond individuals gravitate toward diplomatic responses even when a more direct response would be more developmental.\n\nThis one is the weakest supported of the five. Smither, London and Reilly (2005) review the personality correlates of multisource feedback outcomes at some length and Agreeableness does not appear among them, either positively or negatively. The acquiescence pattern is a clinical observation reported consistently by managers and coaches, not a measured effect, and nobody has quantified the gap between acknowledgement and change as a function of Agreeableness.\n\nFor managers, that argues for a procedural response rather than a diagnostic one: the feedback session that felt most successful, where the recipient was warm, engaged, and seemed genuinely appreciative, is exactly the one whose impact you cannot read from the room. Following up on specific, observable behaviour change after a defined interval is not distrust. It is the only way to find out, for any recipient.\n\n---\n\n## Vision (Openness): Intellectual Engagement, and Whether It Converts\n\nHigh Openness (Vision) individuals engage intellectually with feedback more readily than most. They are curious about themselves, genuinely interested in multiple perspectives, and less threatened by information that challenges their current self-model. They tend to find feedback interesting, which can be, paradoxically, a reception obstacle of its own.\n\nHigh-Vision individuals can process feedback analytically, as an interesting data point about how others perceive them, without fully integrating it affectively. The intellectual engagement is genuine. The personal change may not follow, not because of resistance but because the feedback has been processed at a cognitive level that does not automatically translate into behavioural intention.\n\nThe engagement is not empty, though. In the peer feedback study reviewed by Smither, London and Reilly (2005), Openness was positively related to actual performance improvement, alongside Conscientiousness. Whether analysis converts into change appears to depend on whether the feedback connects to something the recipient already values, which is a property of how the conversation is framed rather than of the trait.\n\n---\n\n## Personality-Specific Feedback Delivery: Five Practical Adaptations\n\nEffective feedback is not a single communication style applied uniformly. It is a flexible practice adjusted to the person receiving it. What follows is practice rather than proof: the adaptations are consistent with the evidence above and cost little to try, and none of them has been tested as an intervention.\n\nFor **high-Depth (Neuroticism)** recipients: establish relational safety before delivering critical content; separate feedback from any implicit evaluation of the person''s worth; allow silence and processing time; avoid urgency language that amplifies threat activation.\n\nFor **very high-Discipline (Conscientiousness)** recipients: frame feedback in process terms rather than outcome terms; always include a clear, specific remediation path; acknowledge strengths explicitly before addressing gaps; avoid language that implies character-level failure.\n\nFor **high-Presence (Extraversion)** recipients: deliver feedback in real-time, face-to-face conversation where possible; allow interaction rather than monologue; follow written feedback with a verbal conversation; keep documentation brief and use it as a reference, not as the primary delivery vehicle.\n\nFor **high-Bond (Agreeableness)** recipients: separate the feedback conversation from any relational warmth signals that could trigger acquiescence; ask for specific commitments to observable behaviour change, not just agreement; follow up on concrete behaviours after a defined interval.\n\nFor **high-Vision (Openness)** recipients: connect feedback to the recipient''s stated goals and values, not just to role expectations; invite the recipient''s own analysis of the gap being addressed; give them ownership of designing the change path.\n\nThe [blind spots research in teams](/blog/blind-spots-in-teams) is a useful companion here, in many cases the feedback that most needs to land is precisely the feedback that addresses the gap between self-perception and peer perception. [Using Cèrcol for team development](/blog/using-cercol-for-team-development-a-practical-guide) covers how to operationalise this in practice.\n\n---\n\n## Summary: Personality Profile, Feedback Challenge, and Delivery Fix\n\n| Personality profile | Feedback challenge | Delivery adaptation |\n|---|---|---|\n| High Depth (Neuroticism) | Negative emotional reaction to feedback that is no less favourable than anyone else''s (Smither et al., 2005); defensive reception and avoidance proposed but unmeasured | Relational safety first; behaviour-focused language; processing time; no urgency |\n| Very high Discipline (Conscientiousness) | Strong follow-through on development; investment in a competent self-image may make critical feedback read as identity threat (hypothesis, not a finding) | Process framing; specific remediation path; strengths acknowledged before gaps |\n| High Presence (Extraversion) | More likely to request further feedback; poorer absorption of written or delayed feedback is plausible but untested | Real-time verbal delivery; interactive conversation; minimal documentation |\n| High Bond (Agreeableness) | Social acquiescence without behaviour change; widely observed, not quantified | Concrete behavioural commitments; follow-up on observable change; resist warm acknowledgement as evidence of integration |\n| High Vision (Openness) | Intellectual engagement that may not convert; positively related to improvement when it does | Goal connection; self-directed change design; ownership of implementation |\n\n---\n\n## Design Feedback Delivery Around Who Actually Receives It\n\nFeedback reception is a recipient problem. Kluger and DeNisi established that a third of feedback interventions backfire, Ilgen and colleagues explained why the recipient is where the leverage sits, and the multisource literature shows that two leaders can receive equally favourable feedback and walk away in entirely different emotional states. And yet most organisations design their feedback processes around what is convenient to deliver, annual reviews, 360-degree questionnaires, post-project retrospectives, with no adjustment for the person receiving it.\n\nCèrcol''s [Witness instrument](/instruments) gives you peer-assessed personality profiles for every team member, so you know in advance whether you are delivering feedback to someone high in Depth, high in Bond, or low in Presence, and can plan the conversation accordingly. Rather than improvising after a session goes wrong, you can structure it deliberately from the start, and then check whether it worked. [See what the Witness instrument measures](/blog/what-the-cercol-witness-instrument-measures) to understand how the peer data is generated and how it differs from self-report. Run your team at [cercol.team](https://cercol.team).\n\n---\n\n*Further reading: [Self-other agreement in the Big Five: where the gaps are biggest](/blog/self-other-agreement-big-five-where-gaps-are-biggest) · [How to give personality-informed feedback](/blog/how-to-give-personality-informed-feedback)*\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [What Is Agreeableness](/blog/what-is-agreeableness-the-cooperative-dimension)\n- [What Is Neuroticism](/blog/what-is-neuroticism-understanding-emotional-depth-at-work)\n- [How to Give Personality-Informed Feedback](/blog/how-to-give-personality-informed-feedback)\n- [Self-Other Agreement in Personality Assessments](/blog/self-other-agreement-big-five-where-gaps-are-biggest)\n- [What Is a High-Bond Team and Why Honest Feedback Is Hard](/blog/too-agreeable-why-high-bond-teams-struggle-with-honest-feedback)\n- [Personality Coaching: Using Big Five as a Development Tool](/blog/personality-coaching-using-big-five-as-development-tool)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Anseel, F., Beatty, A. S., Shen, W., Lievens, F., & Sackett, P. R. (2015). How are we doing after 30 years? A meta-analytic review of the antecedents and outcomes of feedback-seeking behavior. *Journal of Management*, 41(1), 318–348. [doi:10.1177/0149206313484521](https://doi.org/10.1177/0149206313484521)\n- Ashford, S. J., Blatt, R., & VandeWalle, D. (2003). Reflections on the looking glass: A review of research on feedback-seeking behavior in organizations. *Journal of Management*, 29(6), 773–799. [doi:10.1016/S0149-2063(03)00079-5](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0149-2063%2803%2900079-5)\n- Ilgen, D. R., Fisher, C. D., & Taylor, M. S. (1979). Consequences of individual feedback on behavior in organizations. *Journal of Applied Psychology*, 64(4), 349–371. [doi:10.1037/0021-9010.64.4.349](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.64.4.349)\n- Kluger, A. N., & DeNisi, A. (1996). The effects of feedback interventions on performance: A historical review, a meta-analysis, and a preliminary feedback intervention theory. *Psychological Bulletin*, 119(2), 254–284. [doi:10.1037/0033-2909.119.2.254](https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.119.2.254)\n- Smither, J. W., London, M., & Reilly, R. R. (2005). Does performance improve following multisource feedback? A theoretical model, meta-analysis, and review of empirical findings. *Personnel Psychology*, 58(1), 33–66. [doi:10.1111/j.1744-6570.2005.514_1.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1744-6570.2005.514_1.x)\n"'::jsonb),
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE slug = 'personality-and-feedback-reception-why-some-people-reject-feedback';
UPDATE blog_posts SET content = jsonb_set(content, '{en}', '"# Personality and job fit: how to think about person-environment fit\n\nThe intuition that certain personalities suit certain jobs is so widespread that it is practically common sense. We assume extraverts make better salespeople. We expect creative roles to attract open, curious minds. We imagine that surgeons need to be precise, methodical, emotionally controlled.\n\nSome of these intuitions are supported by data. Others are not. And the scientific framework for thinking about personality-job fit, person-environment fit theory, is both more rigorous and more limited than the popular version suggests.\n\nThis article examines what the research actually says, what the main theoretical frameworks offer, and how to think about personality and job fit in a way that helps build better teams without reifying people into types.\n\n## Person-Environment Fit Theory: The Foundation of Personality-Based Hiring\n\n<div class=\"stat-grid\">\n  <div class=\"stat-card\"><div class=\"stat-value\">ρ = 0.56</div><div class=\"stat-label\">Person-job fit → job satisfaction</div></div>\n  <div class=\"stat-card\"><div class=\"stat-value\">ρ = −0.46</div><div class=\"stat-label\">Person-job fit → intent to quit</div></div>\n  <div class=\"stat-card\"><div class=\"stat-value\">ρ = 0.20</div><div class=\"stat-label\">Person-job fit → overall job performance</div></div>\n</div>\n\nPerson-environment (PE) fit is the umbrella term for a family of theories about how the match between individual characteristics and environmental characteristics predicts outcomes like job satisfaction, performance, and retention ([Wikipedia: Person-environment fit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Person%E2%80%93environment_fit)).\n\nThe three figures above come from the largest quantitative synthesis of that literature, a meta-analysis of 172 studies yielding 836 effect sizes ([doi:10.1111/j.1744-6570.2005.00672.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1744-6570.2005.00672.x)). They are corrected correlations (ρ) for person-job fit specifically: 0.56 with job satisfaction, −0.46 with intent to quit, and 0.20 with overall job performance. The gap between the first two numbers and the third is the single most important thing in the fit literature. Fit predicts how people feel about their work far more strongly than it predicts how well they do it, and the authors flag that the credibility interval for the performance relationship is the one exception that includes zero, so even 0.20 generalises less dependably than the attitudinal figures. Person-organisation fit is weaker still against performance, at ρ = 0.07.\n\nWithin that umbrella, the most practically important distinctions are:\n\n- **Person-job fit (PJ fit):** the match between individual abilities and personality and the demands and requirements of a specific job\n- **Person-organisation fit (PO fit):** the match between individual values and personality and the culture and values of the organisation\n- **Person-group fit (PG fit):** the compatibility between an individual and the team they work within\n- **Person-vocation fit (PV fit):** the alignment between personality and broad occupational type, often modelled through Holland''s RIASEC framework\n\nThese are distinct constructs with different predictors and different outcomes, and the meta-analytic numbers separate cleanly: person-group fit correlates ρ = 0.31 with job satisfaction against person-job fit''s 0.56. Conflating them, treating job fit, organisation fit, and team fit as interchangeable, is one of the most common errors in applied talent practice. For a broader treatment of how Big Five traits map to career domains, see [Personality and career choice: what Big Five research actually predicts](/blog/personality-and-career-choice-what-big-five-predicts).\n\n## Holland''s RIASEC Model: What It Gets Right and Where It Fails\n\nThe dominant framework for person-vocation fit is [John Holland''s RIASEC typology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holland_Codes), which classifies both people and work environments into six types: Realistic, Investigative, Artistic, Social, Enterprising, and Conventional. The theory predicts that people are happiest and most productive when their personal type matches their work environment type.\n\nThe empirical evidence is real but modest. The most recent meta-analysis of interest fit, covering 105 studies across more than 65 years (194 samples, 39,602 people), puts the relationship between interest fit and overall job satisfaction at ρ = 0.19, a result its authors call \"slightly lower than expected\" ([doi:10.1016/j.jvb.2020.103503](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jvb.2020.103503)). That is a real effect, and it is also a small one. The typology has well-documented limitations besides:\n\n- It was developed and validated primarily on North American samples and shows weaker cross-cultural validity\n- It treats occupations as having a single dominant type, which ignores intra-occupational variation\n- It provides no mechanism for understanding how a person''s personality affects how they perform within a role, only whether they will find the role satisfying\n- It does not map cleanly onto the Big Five, the more scientifically rigorous framework\n\nThe Big Five approach to job fit is messier than RIASEC but better validated. Rather than assigning people and jobs to types, it asks which specific traits predict which specific outcomes for which specific job families.\n\n## What Big Five Evidence Actually Shows About Personality-Job Fit\n\nThe meta-analytic literature on personality and job performance yields a consistent picture, and the foundational result is still Barrick and Mount''s synthesis across five occupational groups and three performance criteria ([doi:10.1111/j.1744-6570.1991.tb00688.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1744-6570.1991.tb00688.x)): Conscientiousness is the most universal predictor, and its relationship with performance holds across virtually all job types. Other traits show job-type-specific effects. For a deep dive into why Conscientiousness dominates the research, see [What is Conscientiousness? The most consistent predictor of job performance](/blog/what-is-conscientiousness-the-most-consistent-predictor-of-job-performance).\n\n> \"Extraversion was a valid predictor for two occupations involving social interaction, managers and sales (across criterion types). Also, both Openness to Experience and Extraversion were valid predictors of the training proficiency criterion (across occupations).\"\n> - Barrick & Mount (1991), abstract\n\n| Job type | Most relevant Big Five trait (Cèrcol name) | Evidence level |\n|---|---|---|\n| All professional roles | Conscientiousness (Discipline) | Strong, meta-analytic, cross-occupational |\n| Sales and business development | Extraversion (Presence) | Moderate, established meta-analytically for sales and managerial roles |\n| Leadership and management | Extraversion (Presence) + low Neuroticism (low Depth) | Moderate, leadership emergence, not effectiveness |\n| Creative and innovation roles | Openness (Vision) | Moderate, strongest for training proficiency criteria |\n| Customer service and team roles | Agreeableness (Bond) | Weak in the original meta-analysis, better supported for helping behaviour than for task performance |\n| Analytical and research roles | Conscientiousness (Discipline) + Openness (Vision) | Moderate, combined profile |\n| High-stress operational roles | Low Neuroticism (low Depth) | Moderate, varies by occupational group |\n\nTwo important caveats apply to this table. First, it records which traits have shown validity, not how large the effects are. Where the size has been quantified it is small: person-job fit itself correlates only ρ = 0.20 with overall performance, and interest fit only ρ = 0.19 with job satisfaction. Anyone promising you a decisive hiring signal from a trait score is selling something the evidence does not contain. Second, these are average effects across many studies; individual job families within a category may show different patterns. For a specific role deep-dive, see [Software engineer personality: what research shows](/blog/software-engineer-personality-what-research-shows) or [Sales personality: what traits predict sales performance](/blog/sales-personality-what-traits-predict-sales-performance).\n\n## Fit vs. Adaptability: Why Hiring for Fit Can Backfire\n\nOne of the most interesting debates in the fit literature is whether fit actually optimises performance or whether it is merely correlated with comfort. The meta-analytic pattern is at least suggestive on this point: fit is strongly related to satisfaction and to staying, and only weakly related to performing. The adaptability perspective argues that what matters most is not the static match between trait and job demand, but the individual''s capacity to adjust their behaviour across different situations.\n\nThe evidence is mixed, but there is a compelling argument that over-optimising for fit can produce brittle teams. A team of introverts in a customer-facing role will struggle to adapt when the demands shift. A team of very high-Agreeableness individuals may lack the productive conflict needed for rigorous decision-making.\n\nThis suggests that thinking about personality fit at the team level, rather than the individual level, is more powerful. The question is not \"does this person fit this job?\" but \"does this person add something to this team profile that makes the team more effective?\"\n\n## Why Team-Level Personality Fit Matters More Than Individual Fit\n\nThe person-group fit literature suggests that team composition effects on performance are real and meaningful, if modest: person-group fit correlates ρ = 0.31 with job satisfaction and ρ = 0.19 with overall performance ([doi:10.1111/j.1744-6570.2005.00672.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1744-6570.2005.00672.x)). Heterogeneous teams, teams with a wider range of personality profiles, tend to outperform homogeneous teams on complex tasks that require different cognitive styles. But this relationship is moderated by team processes: heterogeneous teams need more structure and clearer communication norms to realise their potential.\n\nSee also: [Should you hire for personality fit or personality diversity?](/blog/should-you-hire-for-personality-fit-or-personality-diversity).\n\nThe practical implication is that the right question when adding a person to a team is not \"are they high enough in Conscientiousness?\" but \"how does their profile interact with the existing team composition?\" A team already saturated with high-Discipline, low-Vision individuals might gain more from someone who brings challenge and creative friction than from another conscientious executor. The [12 Cèrcol team roles](/blog/the-12-cercol-team-roles-explained) offer one evidence-based vocabulary for this kind of team-level analysis.\n\n## How Cèrcol Uses Big Five Profiles for Team-Level Fit Assessment\n\nCèrcol does not provide a \"fit score\", a single number that tells you whether a person matches a role. This is a deliberate choice. A fit score would be scientifically unjustifiable (there is no universal personality profile for any non-trivial job), practically misleading (fit is multidimensional, not scalar), and potentially discriminatory (it would effectively screen candidates on personality). The legal constraints around personality-based selection are examined in detail in [Personality testing in hiring: what is legal and what is ethical?](/blog/personality-testing-in-hiring-what-is-legal-what-is-ethical).\n\nInstead, Cèrcol shows the full profile, all five dimensions, in both self-report and [Witness peer assessment](/instruments) data, and lets teams interpret that profile in the context of their specific situation. The [Roles](/roles) section provides evidence-based guidance on which traits are most relevant for which role types, anchored to the peer-reviewed literature.\n\nThe goal is to give teams the information they need to have better conversations about fit, adaptability, and complementarity, not to replace those conversations with an algorithm. Person-environment fit is real. Its effects on performance are modest. And it is far more usefully discussed as a team question than answered as an individual score.\n\n---\n\n## Put Fit Theory Into Practice\n\nFit is most useful as a conversational tool, not a gatekeeping number. Cèrcol gives every team member a full Big Five profile, self-report plus Witness perspective, and maps that profile to the [12 team roles](/roles) that reflect how personality shapes contribution in practice. The result is a shared language for discussing where each person creates energy and where friction accumulates.\n\nWhether you are thinking about your own next career move or building a team from scratch, the most actionable step is seeing the full picture. [Get your free Cèrcol profile](https://cercol.team), free, scientifically grounded, and built for team conversations, not personality typing.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Kristof-Brown, A. L., Zimmerman, R. D., & Johnson, E. C. (2005). Consequences of individuals'' fit at work: A meta-analysis of person-job, person-organization, person-group, and person-supervisor fit. *Personnel Psychology*, 58(2), 281–342. [doi:10.1111/j.1744-6570.2005.00672.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1744-6570.2005.00672.x)\n- Hoff, K. A., Song, Q. C., Wee, C. J. M., Phan, W. M. J., & Rounds, J. (2020). Interest fit and job satisfaction: A systematic review and meta-analysis. *Journal of Vocational Behavior*, 123, 103503. [doi:10.1016/j.jvb.2020.103503](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jvb.2020.103503)\n- Barrick, M. R., & Mount, M. K. (1991). The Big Five personality dimensions and job performance: A meta-analysis. *Personnel Psychology*, 44(1), 1–26. [doi:10.1111/j.1744-6570.1991.tb00688.x](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1744-6570.1991.tb00688.x)\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [Personality and career choice: what Big Five research predicts](/blog/personality-and-career-choice-what-big-five-predicts)\n- [Job satisfaction and personality: what the research shows](/blog/job-satisfaction-personality-what-predicts-it)\n- [How personality predicts onboarding success](/blog/how-personality-predicts-onboarding-success)\n- [Should you hire for personality fit or personality diversity?](/blog/should-you-hire-for-personality-fit-or-personality-diversity)\n- [Personality testing in hiring: what is legal and what is ethical?](/blog/personality-testing-in-hiring-what-is-legal-what-is-ethical)\n- [Personality and motivation: what drives each Big Five profile](/blog/personality-and-motivation-what-drives-each-big-five-profile-what-drives-each-big-five-profile)\n"'::jsonb),
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE slug = 'personality-and-job-fit-how-to-think-about-person-environment-fit';
UPDATE blog_posts SET content = jsonb_set(content, '{en}', '"# Personality and negotiation: who wins, and why\n\n[Negotiation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negotiation) is one of the most studied interpersonal behaviours in psychology, economics, and management science. It is also one of the most personality-sensitive in the popular imagination: the same situation is supposed to produce radically different outcomes depending on who is at the table.\n\nThe research is more deflationary than that. Big Five work on negotiation is far less extensive than the literature on job performance or leadership, and the most comprehensive recent review of individual differences in negotiation reports that most personality traits appear to have a minimal influence on objective performance, with one reliable exception: Extraversion and Agreeableness tend to be liabilities in strictly competitive situations ([doi:10.1177/0963721414558114](https://doi.org/10.1177/0963721414558114)). What personality shapes more dependably is how negotiators experience the process, which situations suit them, and how much the outcome depends on preparation they did before sitting down. Those are still worth understanding, both for predicting your own tendencies and for anticipating those of the person across the table.\n\n---\n\n## The Dual Concern Model: Assertiveness vs. Cooperativeness in Negotiation\n\nThe most widely used framework in negotiation research is the [dual concern model](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dual_concern_model), which organises negotiation styles along two axes: assertiveness (concern for one''s own outcomes) and cooperativeness (concern for the other party''s outcomes). The four resulting quadrants, competing, collaborating, accommodating, and avoiding, each predict different negotiation behaviours and outcomes.\n\nPersonality is usually assumed to map onto this framework in the obvious way, with high Presence (Extraversion) and low Bond (Agreeableness) on the assertive side and high Bond on the cooperative side. Treat that as the theory''s prediction rather than as a measured finding: it is the mapping the model implies, not a coefficient anyone has established. What the outcome data do show is that being at an extreme of either dimension is not what distinguishes effective negotiators.\n\nThis is the central insight that naive trait-based accounts of negotiation miss: raw assertiveness does not win negotiations. Sophisticated negotiators know when to compete, when to collaborate, when to accommodate strategically, and when to take a break. This contextual flexibility requires not just the right trait profile, but the self-awareness to recognise which mode the situation calls for.\n\nUnderstanding your own profile across the five dimensions is the prerequisite for this kind of flexibility, see the [12 Cèrcol team roles explained](/blog/the-12-cercol-team-roles-explained) for how trait combinations translate into team behaviour patterns, some of which map directly onto negotiation styles.\n\n---\n\n## Extraversion and Negotiation Confidence: Bold Openers and Their Limits\n\nHigh Presence (Extraversion) confers real interpersonal advantages in negotiation contexts: verbal fluency, comfort with interpersonal pressure, willingness to make assertive opening offers, and resilience when the negotiation becomes adversarial. Extraverted negotiators are less likely to concede early out of discomfort with silence or tension.\n\nWhat that does not do is put more money on their side of the table. In the study that first tested the full five-factor model in bargaining simulations, Extraversion correlated −0.26 with the buyer''s own economic gain, and the effect was strongest among negotiators who had not set an ambitious target beforehand ([doi:10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345)).\n\n> \"The authors hypothesized and found evidence that Extraversion and Agreeableness are liabilities in distributive bargaining encounters.\"\n> - Barry & Friedman (1998), abstract\n\nThe plausible mechanism is overconfidence. High-Extraversion negotiators may rely too heavily on social performance, charm, assertiveness, verbal dominance, and underinvest in the analytical preparation that creates genuine negotiation power. The well-prepared introvert with a detailed understanding of BATNA (Best Alternative to a Negotiated Agreement), reservation prices, and the other party''s interests is not fighting an uphill battle here.\n\nFor a deeper account of how Extraversion shapes professional behaviour beyond negotiation, see [what is Extraversion, beyond the introvert–extrovert binary](/blog/what-is-extraversion-beyond-the-introvert-extrovert-binary).\n\n---\n\n## Agreeableness and Concession-Making: Why High-Bond Negotiators Leave Money Behind\n\nBond (Agreeableness) is the trait most often blamed for negotiation vulnerability. High-Bond individuals dislike interpersonal conflict, value harmonious relationships, and find the assertive pressure of negotiation genuinely uncomfortable. The folk account translates that into concrete behaviours: earlier concessions, larger concessions, lower opening offers, and premature acceptance of proposals that fall short of what was achievable.\n\nThe measured picture is more conditional. In Barry and Friedman''s simulations the simple correlation between Agreeableness and economic gain was close to zero (−0.07 for buyers, −0.06 for sellers). The disadvantage showed up as an interaction: personality effects were more pronounced in the absence of high aspirations ([doi:10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345)). A later two-study test reframed the same result as person-situation fit, finding that negotiators high in Agreeableness were best suited to integrative negotiations and negotiators low in Agreeableness were best suited to distributive ones ([doi:10.1037/a0025706](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0025706)). For the full research base on what Agreeableness costs in competitive professional contexts, see [what is Agreeableness, the cooperative dimension](/blog/what-is-agreeableness-the-cooperative-dimension).\n\nSo the honest version of the claim is narrower than the popular one. High Bond is not a general handicap at the table. It is a poor match for the purely distributive part of a negotiation, and the mismatch is largest for people who walked in without a target.\n\nThat points at a structural remedy rather than a dispositional one: prepare assertive positions in advance, set explicit floors and ceilings before entering the conversation, and rely on pre-committed standards rather than in-the-moment judgment about what feels fair. This converts the negotiation from a live emotional experience, which high Bond makes difficult, to the execution of a pre-made plan. It is also the intervention the aspiration finding most directly supports.\n\n---\n\n## Conscientiousness and Preparation: The Negotiation Advantage of Discipline\n\nPreparation is the part of negotiation where Discipline (Conscientiousness) ought to matter most. High-Discipline negotiators research their counterpart''s interests, establish their own reservation price and BATNA before entering, identify potential issues beyond the primary one (creating room for log-rolling and value creation), and document agreements precisely.\n\nThe evidence for that ought is thin, and it is worth saying so plainly. Barry and Friedman reported that, contrary to predictions, Conscientiousness was generally unrelated to bargaining success; at the dyad level it correlated −0.10 with joint utility and 0.01 with integrativeness ([doi:10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345)). The recent review of individual differences reaches the same conclusion and offers the most likely explanation: Conscientiousness has had no documented influence, but most of the research has been conducted in laboratory settings where preparation is constrained ([doi:10.1177/0963721414558114](https://doi.org/10.1177/0963721414558114)).\n\nThat is a genuine gap rather than a refutation. A simulation that hands both parties the same briefing packet twenty minutes before the exercise has designed away the advantage Discipline is supposed to confer. Until field studies close that gap, the preparation story is a reasonable hypothesis, not a finding.\n\n| Big Five trait | Cèrcol name | Negotiation strength | Negotiation risk |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Extraversion | Presence | Opening offer confidence, pressure tolerance, verbal fluency | Measured liability in distributive bargaining, overconfidence, under-preparation |\n| Agreeableness | Bond | Relationship building, trust creation, better suited to integrative encounters | Poor fit for distributive encounters, especially without a pre-set target |\n| Conscientiousness | Discipline | Preparation quality, follow-through, precise documentation | No documented effect on laboratory bargaining outcomes |\n| Neuroticism (low) | Low Depth | Threat regulation, composure under pressure, patience | |\n| High Neuroticism | High Depth | | Threat response escalation, harder BATNA assessment under pressure |\n| Openness | Vision | Willingness to reframe and generate options | No measured advantage for joint outcomes |\n\n---\n\n## Neuroticism and Threat Response: How Anxiety Undermines Negotiation\n\nDepth (Neuroticism) is usually described as shaping negotiation behaviour through the threat-response mechanism. Negotiations routinely involve pressure, ambiguity, and moments of interpersonal friction, the exact conditions that activate stress responses in high-Depth individuals. Under activation of the threat system, attention narrows, time horizons shorten, and the cognitive bandwidth needed for strategic thinking diminishes.\n\nThis is a mechanism story, and it should be labelled as one. In the bargaining data, emotional stability was essentially unrelated to economic gain for both roles ([doi:10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345)), so the widely repeated claim that anxious negotiators systematically concede more is not something the outcome research has established. What is documented is the more general finding that traits shape how negotiators feel about an encounter far more reliably than what they extract from it ([doi:10.1177/0963721414558114](https://doi.org/10.1177/0963721414558114)).\n\nThe practical intervention for high-Depth negotiators parallels that for high-Bond negotiators: externalise the decision-making as much as possible through advance commitment. Set your floor before entering. Define the conditions under which you will accept or reject a proposal. Treat the negotiation as execution of a previously made decision rather than a live emotional assessment. This reduces the surface area over which threat activation can corrupt the process, whatever its size turns out to be.\n\nThe same avoidance-motivation pattern that makes high-Depth individuals uncomfortable under negotiation pressure also shapes how they communicate and receive feedback, see [personality and communication style, direct vs diplomatic](/blog/personality-and-communication-style-direct-vs-diplomatic) for how this plays out across different interaction contexts.\n\n---\n\n## Openness and Creative Problem-Solving in Integrative Negotiation\n\nVision (Openness) is the trait most often credited with producing creative, integrative agreements. Many negotiations that appear to be zero-sum, fixed-pie, are actually integrative: there are ways to expand the value available by combining issues, sequencing concessions strategically, or finding solutions that were not on the original table. Discovering those solutions requires thinking flexibly about the problem and reframing the situation in ways that reveal previously invisible value, which sounds exactly like Openness.\n\nThe data do not cooperate. In the one study to test it directly at the dyad level, aggregated Openness correlated −0.31 with the integrativeness of the settlement, a negative relationship, and 0.01 with joint utility. What predicted integrative outcomes was cognitive ability: 0.40 with joint utility and 0.50 with integrativeness ([doi:10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345)). The broader review agrees that abilities, not traits, are what reliably improve win-win outcomes ([doi:10.1177/0963721414558114](https://doi.org/10.1177/0963721414558114)).\n\nThe honest reading is that expanding the pie looks like a problem-solving task rather than a personality one. It is about how the parties engage with the structure of the problem, not how curious or imaginative they are. High-Vision negotiators may well ask \"what if we approached this differently?\" more often. Asking the question is not the same as finding the answer.\n\n---\n\n## Why Low Agreeableness Alone Doesn''t Win Negotiations\n\nA persistent misconception in the popular negotiation literature is that toughness, low Agreeableness, assertiveness, willingness to walk away, is the primary determinant of negotiation success. The research does not support this either.\n\nLow Bond suits distributive encounters and high Bond suits integrative ones ([doi:10.1037/a0025706](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0025706)), which means neither is an all-purpose advantage. The negotiation literature distinguishes between claiming value and creating value; the most skilled negotiators do both, in the right sequence, and low Bond by itself helps only with the claiming, in the encounters that are purely about claiming.\n\n---\n\n## What Personality ''Disadvantaged'' Negotiators Can Actually Do\n\nThe practically important question for many readers is not what personality traits predict negotiation success in the aggregate, but what people with an unhelpful profile for a given encounter can do about it.\n\nHere the research is unusually encouraging, because the factors with the strongest documented effects are the changeable ones. The review of individual differences reports that positive expectations and comfort with negotiating are the most reliable predictors of performance, and that self-efficacy had the strongest effect of any single variable tested across all categories of individual difference ([doi:10.1177/0963721414558114](https://doi.org/10.1177/0963721414558114)). Alongside that sits the aspiration finding: personality effects on bargaining outcomes were more pronounced in the absence of high aspirations ([doi:10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345)). Setting an ambitious, specific target before you walk in is the closest thing the literature offers to a dispositional equaliser.\n\nSo the advice for the high-Bond negotiator who commits to specific floors before the conversation, or the high-Depth negotiator who builds in a break before any final commitment, is not that these tactics have been shown to close a measured performance gap. It is that the traits themselves matter less than the preparation, the target, and the expectation you bring, and those three are yours to set.\n\nThis kind of profile-informed preparation is exactly what [personality coaching, using Big Five as a development tool](/blog/personality-coaching-using-big-five-as-development-tool) trains people to do. And because negotiation outcomes are influenced by both parties'' profiles and by the fit between disposition and situation, being able to read the encounter matters as much as knowing yourself, see [self-other agreement in Big Five, where the gaps are biggest](/blog/self-other-agreement-big-five-where-gaps-are-biggest) for how perception and reality diverge in ways that matter at the table.\n\n---\n\n## Know Your Negotiation Style Before You Sit Down\n\nEvery negotiation involves two personality profiles, not one. Understanding your own default tendencies, where you are inclined to over-concede, where you under-prepare, where discomfort narrows your thinking, is a structural advantage that no amount of in-the-moment improvisation provides.\n\n**Cèrcol maps your Big Five profile across Discipline, Presence, Bond, Depth, and Vision, showing you where your negotiation tendencies are likely to help and where they are likely to cost you.** Take the free assessment at [cercol.team](https://cercol.team) and identify your profile before your next important negotiation. If you work in a team where internal negotiations are as consequential as external ones, understanding your colleagues'' profiles adds another layer of strategic awareness that most teams never develop.\n\n---\n\n*Further reading: [Agreeableness at work: the hidden cost of being too nice](/blog/agreeableness-at-work-the-hidden-cost-of-being-too-nice) · [Personality and decision-making: how Big Five shapes judgment](/blog/personality-and-decision-making-how-big-five-shapes-judgment)*\n\n## Sources\n\n- Barry, B., & Friedman, R. A. (1998). Bargainer characteristics in distributive and integrative negotiation. *Journal of Personality and Social Psychology*, 74(2), 345–359. [doi:10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.74.2.345)\n- Dimotakis, N., Conlon, D. E., & Ilies, R. (2012). The mind and heart (literally) of the negotiator: Personality and contextual determinants of experiential reactions and economic outcomes in negotiation. *Journal of Applied Psychology*, 97(1), 183–193. [doi:10.1037/a0025706](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0025706)\n- Elfenbein, H. A. (2015). Individual differences in negotiation: A nearly abandoned pursuit revived. *Current Directions in Psychological Science*, 24(2), 131–136. [doi:10.1177/0963721414558114](https://doi.org/10.1177/0963721414558114)\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [What Is Agreeableness](/blog/what-is-agreeableness-the-cooperative-dimension)\n- [What Is Extraversion](/blog/what-is-extraversion-beyond-the-introvert-extrovert-binary)\n- [Sales Personality: What Traits Predict Sales Performance](/blog/sales-personality-what-traits-predict-sales-performance)\n- [Conflict Resolution Styles: Personality](/blog/conflict-resolution-styles-personality)\n- [Personality and Decision-Making: How Big Five Shapes Judgment](/blog/personality-and-decision-making-how-big-five-shapes-judgment)\n- [Low Agreeableness in Leadership: When Being Difficult Works](/blog/low-agreeableness-in-leadership-when-directness-helps-and-when-it-harms)\n"'::jsonb),
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE slug = 'personality-and-negotiation-who-wins-and-why';
UPDATE blog_posts SET content = jsonb_set(content, '{en}', '"# Trust in teams: the personality foundations of team cohesion\n\nAsk anyone who has worked in both high-trust and low-trust teams what the difference felt like, and the answers converge on the same themes. In high-trust teams, people say things they actually think. They admit uncertainty. They raise problems before they become crises. They do not perform competence at the cost of accuracy. In low-trust teams, the same activities feel dangerous. People hedge. They protect themselves. They manage impressions rather than problems.\n\nThe structural importance of trust in organisational settings is well established. Edmondson''s foundational work on psychological safety, a closely related construct, found that team psychological safety, \"a shared belief held by members of a team that the team is safe for interpersonal risk taking\", is associated with the learning behaviour that mediates between safety and team performance ([doi:10.2307/2666999](https://doi.org/10.2307/2666999)). But trust is not a single thing. The academic literature distinguishes between at least two types of trust, each playing a different role in team functioning.\n\nUnderstanding those types, and understanding how personality relates to them, is essential for any serious attempt to build and maintain team cohesion. It also means being careful about how much the personality evidence will actually bear, which is less than the popular treatments suggest.\n\n---\n\n## Cognitive Trust vs. Affective Trust: The Team Trust Distinction\n\nThe distinction that organises this literature comes from McAllister''s field study of 194 managers and professionals, which separated trust \"from the head\" from trust that develops as two people interact over time ([doi:10.2307/256727](https://doi.org/10.2307/256727)). A useful general overview of the trust literature is available at [Wikipedia: Trust (social science)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trust_(social_science)).\n\n**Cognitive trust**, sometimes called competence-based trust, is the belief that someone is capable, reliable, and will follow through on their commitments. It is calculative: it develops through demonstrated track records, shared information about expertise, and accumulated evidence of reliability over time. Cognitive trust enables task coordination. When you trust a colleague cognitively, you delegate to them, rely on their assessments, and do not spend energy double-checking their work.\n\n**Affective trust**, sometimes called goodwill-based trust, is the belief that someone genuinely cares about your interests, will not exploit your vulnerabilities, and is emotionally safe to be honest with. It is relational: it develops through expressions of care, consistent emotional attunement, and the experience of being treated well when you were vulnerable. Affective trust enables honest communication. When you trust a colleague affectively, you share your actual concerns, admit your mistakes, and ask for help rather than performing competence.\n\nBoth types are necessary for high-functioning teams. Cognitive trust without affective trust produces efficient but brittle teams, people coordinate well but do not share problems until they become unavoidable. Affective trust without cognitive trust produces warm but unproductive teams, people feel safe being honest but do not actually believe each other is competent enough for their assessments to matter.\n\n---\n\n## How High Agreeableness (Bond) Initiates, and Distorts, Team Trust\n\nThe Big Five trait most often linked to trust is Agreeableness, Bond in Cèrcol''s framework. Trust is, in most standard Big Five instruments, literally one of the facets used to measure Agreeableness, so a relationship between the two is close to definitional. High-Bond individuals show higher baseline trust propensity: they are more willing to make initial investments of trust before they have evidence to justify them. They are also more likely to signal trustworthiness to others, through warmth, cooperation, and demonstrated concern for others'' interests.\n\nTwo things are worth saying about how strong that link is, because it is routinely overstated. First, the trust literature separates trust propensity, \"a dispositional willingness to rely on others\", from trust itself, the intention to accept vulnerability based on positive expectations, and the meta-analytic evidence shows propensity contributing incremental variance to behavioural outcomes rather than determining them ([doi:10.1037/0021-9010.92.4.909](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.92.4.909)). Second, when personality is tested against actual behaviour in trust-relevant situations rather than against questionnaires, the effects are small. The largest meta-analysis of personality and prosocial behaviour, covering 770 studies and 3,523 effects across six economic games including the Trust Game, reports correlations spanning −0.18 to 0.26 ([doi:10.1037/bul0000217](https://doi.org/10.1037/bul0000217)). Nobody should be quoting you a large coefficient linking Agreeableness to trust.\n\nThe team-level dynamic is still worth attending to. In teams with wide Agreeableness variance, high-Bond members extend trust early and broadly while low-Bond members extend it slowly and conditionally. Neither is irrational. But the asymmetry generates a trust mismatch: the high-Bond member has made a relational investment that the low-Bond member has not yet reciprocated, creating a felt imbalance that can erode the high-Bond member''s willingness to keep investing. When this extends to team-wide feedback culture, high-Bond teams can become [too agreeable to surface honest assessment](/blog/too-agreeable-why-high-bond-teams-struggle-with-honest-feedback), a failure mode worth understanding alongside trust dynamics.\n\n---\n\n## How Conscientiousness (Discipline) Builds Reliable Cognitive Trust\n\nConscientiousness (Discipline) is the personality dimension most naturally connected to cognitive, competence-based trust, which McAllister characterises as a judgment grounded in evidence of another''s competence and reliability ([doi:10.2307/256727](https://doi.org/10.2307/256727)). High-Discipline individuals are reliable, organised, and follow through on commitments. They are the people you can give a task to without building in oversight. Over time, this behavioural consistency generates a track record that supports confident delegation and reduced monitoring costs.\n\nNote the shape of that argument: it runs from Conscientiousness to reliable behaviour, and from reliable behaviour to the evidence cognitive trust is built on. That is a mechanism, not a measured coefficient, and we have not found a meta-analytic estimate of the Conscientiousness-to-trust path worth quoting. Treat it as the best available reading of two well-supported literatures meeting, rather than as a number.\n\nThe relevant risk for high-Discipline teams is perfectionism-driven trust erosion. When standards are extremely demanding, team members may become reluctant to commit to deadlines they cannot meet with certainty, which paradoxically reduces the reliability signals that cognitive trust depends on. This dynamic also appears in the [Vision-Discipline tension](/blog/the-vision-discipline-tension-innovation-vs-execution), where high-Discipline standards can create friction with more exploratory teammates.\n\n---\n\n## How High Neuroticism (Depth) Disrupts Trust Through Threat Sensitivity\n\nNeuroticism, Depth in Cèrcol''s framework, is the trait most commonly associated with trust disruption. High-Depth individuals show heightened threat sensitivity: they are more likely to interpret ambiguous social signals as threatening, more likely to recall negative interpersonal experiences, and more reactive to perceived betrayals of trust.\n\nThis creates a feedback loop in team contexts. High-Depth team members may extend trust more cautiously, which limits the relational data they accumulate about others'' trustworthiness. They may also be more likely to detect genuine micro-signals of untrustworthiness, since the same vigilance that generates false alarms picks up real signals. The commonly asserted net effect on team functioning is negative: higher monitoring, lower psychological safety, greater tendency to attribute mistakes to malicious intent rather than ordinary human error.\n\nWe could not find a defensible effect size for that net claim, and we are not going to invent one. What the evidence supports is the narrower statement: trust propensity varies between people, it has measurable but incremental effects on trust-relevant behaviour ([doi:10.1037/0021-9010.92.4.909](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.92.4.909)), and personality-to-behaviour relationships in this domain are consistently small ([doi:10.1037/bul0000217](https://doi.org/10.1037/bul0000217)). None of this makes high-Depth team members less trustworthy. It makes trust with them slower to build and, in the experience of most team leaders, more vulnerable to ordinary team friction that low-Depth members would experience as non-events. For a deeper look at how this dimension operates, see [what Neuroticism means at work](/blog/what-is-neuroticism-understanding-emotional-depth-at-work).\n\n---\n\n## Vision (Openness): Intellectual Trust Across the Affective Gap\n\nThe relationship between Openness (Vision) and trust is the thinnest of the five, and the honest summary is that it is largely unmeasured. The plausible account is that high-Vision individuals show high intellectual trust, openness to ideas that differ from their own, willingness to be persuaded by good arguments regardless of source, tolerance for uncertainty in judgment, and that this intellectual openness does not necessarily translate into interpersonal trust of the affective kind.\n\nThat is a hypothesis. It follows from what Openness is understood to measure, and it matches what many team leaders report, but we have not found a study establishing the split between intellectual and affective trust as a function of Openness. Presented as a research finding it would be a fabrication; presented as a lens for interpreting a team profile, it is a reasonable one to hold loosely.\n\nIf it holds, it means Vision diversity can create an interesting trust asymmetry: high-Vision members feeling confident about delegating intellectual work across trust boundaries while maintaining guardedness about emotional disclosure. This pattern connects closely to the [conflict resolution styles](/blog/conflict-resolution-styles-personality) that different personality profiles default to under pressure.\n\n---\n\n## Trust Repair After Betrayal: What Personality Shapes Recovery\n\nOne of the most practically important areas of trust research concerns what happens after trust is violated. The clinical picture from the trust repair literature is sobering: it takes considerably more positive experience to rebuild trust after a betrayal than it took to build it in the first place, and damage propagates faster than repair. That asymmetry is widely reported and widely observed. We have not found a meta-analytic estimate of its size, so no coefficient appears here.\n\nPersonality is generally held to moderate trust repair. High-Bond individuals are described as more likely to forgive and reinvest after a betrayal, especially when it is attributed to situational factors rather than character; high-Depth individuals as having the most difficulty, since a violation is encoded more durably and negative affectivity makes it harder to genuinely re-extend trust even after conscious resolution. Both patterns follow from what the traits are, and both remain, on the evidence we could verify, clinical observation rather than established quantity.\n\nFor team leaders, the practical implication survives the caveat: the personality composition of your team shapes how recoverable your team is from trust-damaging events. A team with high mean Depth may need more structured repair processes, explicit conversations about what happened and what changes, than a team with low mean Depth, where informal repair may be sufficient. [Retrospectives that are personality-aware](/blog/retrospectives-personality-making-them-work) offer one structured format for exactly this kind of repair conversation.\n\n---\n\n## Big Five Dimensions, Trust Types, and Practical Team Implications\n\nThe table below summarises the working model. Read the trust-type column as the mechanism each dimension is most plausibly connected to, not as five separately validated findings.\n\n| Big Five trait | Cèrcol dimension | Trust type | Team impact |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Agreeableness | Bond | Affective / goodwill trust | High-Bond members initiate and sustain relational trust; mismatch with low-Bond creates asymmetric investment |\n| Conscientiousness | Discipline | Cognitive / competence trust | High-Discipline members build reliable track records; very high Discipline may reduce warmth signals |\n| Neuroticism | Depth | Trust disruption | High-Depth members are slower to build trust with; more reactive to trust violations; slower to repair |\n| Openness | Vision | Intellectual trust | Plausibly extends idea-level trust readily; no established effect on affective trust |\n| Extraversion | Presence | Trust signalling | High-Presence members signal confidence and availability; can short-circuit trust-building by projecting premature certainty |\n\n---\n\n## How Peer Assessment Surfaces Trust Gaps Before They Compound\n\nTrust is difficult to measure directly: it is a latent state that manifests in behaviour rather than self-report. Cèrcol''s Witness-based assessment model approaches this indirectly: by gathering multiple independent perspectives on personality, it surfaces the relational data that trust depends on. Teams can see where Bond scores cluster, where Depth variance is high, and where cognitive trust signals are being generated or missing.\n\nThis is not a replacement for the slow work of building trust through repeated interaction, and it is not a trust score. But it makes the structural preconditions, and risks, for trust development visible, which is the essential first step.\n\n---\n\n## Measure Where Your Team''s Trust Actually Comes From\n\nThe personality dimensions that shape trust, Bond, Discipline, and Depth in particular, are not equally visible to team members through ordinary interaction. Self-perception often diverges from peer perception, and [the gaps between the two are where trust problems tend to originate](/blog/self-other-agreement-big-five-where-gaps-are-biggest). Cèrcol''s [Witness instrument](/instruments) was designed specifically to capture these discrepancies: each team member is rated by multiple peers, and the aggregated data reveals where someone''s trust signals are landing differently from how they intend. Because Witnesses rate independently and anonymously, the assessment is more resistant to the social pressures that cause direct feedback to be softened. If your team is navigating trust challenges, or wants to understand its structural trust risks before they compound, the [free assessment at cercol.team](https://cercol.team) is the starting point.\n\n---\n\n*Further reading: [Building psychological safety through personality science](/blog/building-psychological-safety-personality-science) · [Conflict resolution styles and personality](/blog/conflict-resolution-styles-personality)*\n\n## Sources\n\n- McAllister, D. J. (1995). Affect- and cognition-based trust as foundations for interpersonal cooperation in organizations. *Academy of Management Journal*, 38(1), 24–59. [doi:10.2307/256727](https://doi.org/10.2307/256727)\n- Edmondson, A. C. (1999). Psychological safety and learning behavior in work teams. *Administrative Science Quarterly*, 44(2), 350–383. [doi:10.2307/2666999](https://doi.org/10.2307/2666999)\n- Colquitt, J. A., Scott, B. A., & LePine, J. A. (2007). Trust, trustworthiness, and trust propensity: A meta-analytic test of their unique relationships with risk taking and job performance. *Journal of Applied Psychology*, 92(4), 909–927. [doi:10.1037/0021-9010.92.4.909](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.92.4.909)\n- Thielmann, I., Spadaro, G., & Balliet, D. (2020). Personality and prosocial behavior: A theoretical framework and meta-analysis. *Psychological Bulletin*, 146(1), 30–90. [doi:10.1037/bul0000217](https://doi.org/10.1037/bul0000217)\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [Building Psychological Safety: Personality Science](/blog/building-psychological-safety-personality-science)\n- [How to Build a Balanced Team: Personality Perspective](/blog/how-to-build-a-balanced-team)\n- [Personality Conflict in Teams: What It Actually Looks Like](/blog/personality-conflict-in-teams-what-it-actually-looks-like)\n- [What Is Agreeableness](/blog/what-is-agreeableness-the-cooperative-dimension)\n- [Self-Other Agreement in the Big Five: Where Gaps Are Biggest](/blog/self-other-agreement-big-five-where-gaps-are-biggest)\n- [Why Self-Assessment Alone Isn''t Enough](/blog/why-self-assessment-alone-isnt-enough-peer-personality-feedback)\n"'::jsonb),
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE slug = 'trust-in-teams-personality-foundations';

DO $$
DECLARE bad integer;
BEGIN
  SELECT count(*) INTO bad FROM blog_posts WHERE jsonb_typeof(content) <> 'object';
  IF bad > 0 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'blog_posts: % row(s) with a non-object content', bad; END IF;

  SELECT count(*) INTO bad FROM blog_posts, unnest(ARRAY['en','ca','es','fr','de','da']) l
   WHERE length(content->>l) < 500;
  IF bad > 0 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'blog_posts: % row-language pair(s) with a body under 500 chars', bad; END IF;

  -- The rule this migration exists to restore: an English article that shows
  -- a stat card must cite something.
  --
  -- Scoped to the nine articles this migration rewrites, because the first
  -- run of it refused over a tenth. The sweep that picked the nine matched
  -- '<div class="stat-value">' and best-free-personality-tests-for-teams-2026
  -- writes '<span class="stat-value">', so it was missed. This assertion
  -- found it, which is the argument for asserting the rule rather than
  -- trusting the query that built the work list. That article is corrected
  -- separately and 074 widens this check to the whole corpus.
  SELECT count(*) INTO bad FROM blog_posts
   WHERE slug IN ('16personalities-vs-big-five-the-viral-test-that-gets-it-half-right',
                  'anonymity-in-personality-assessment-why-it-matters',
                  'can-you-fake-a-personality-test',
                  'conflict-resolution-styles-personality',
                  'do-generational-differences-in-personality-actually-exist',
                  'personality-and-feedback-reception-why-some-people-reject-feedback',
                  'personality-and-job-fit-how-to-think-about-person-environment-fit',
                  'personality-and-negotiation-who-wins-and-why',
                  'trust-in-teams-personality-foundations')
     AND content->>'en' LIKE '%stat-value%'
     AND content->>'en' !~ '10\.[0-9]{4,9}/';
  IF bad > 0 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'blog_posts: % English article(s) with a stat card and no DOI', bad; END IF;
END $$;

COMMIT;
